// The Good Wife S1E01-E08 transcripts compiled for personal language study from public subtitle/transcript sources (fair use). Sources: springfieldspringfield.co.uk, subslikescript.com, tvsubs.net
window.TRANSCRIPTS = {
  "S01E01": [
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Good morning." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "An hour ago, I resigned as State's Attorney of Cook County." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "I did this with a heavy heart and a deep commitment to fight these scurrilous charges." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "I want to be clear. I have never abused my office." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "I have never traded lighter sentences for financial or sexual favors." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "At the same time, I need to atone for my personal failings with my wife, Alicia, and our two children." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "The money used in these transactions was mine and mine alone. No public funds were ever utilized." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "But I do admit to a failure of judgment in my private dealings with these women." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Alicia and I ask that the press please respect our privacy. Give us time to heal." },
    { "sp": "Reporter", "t": "Sir! Are you still involved with prostitutes, sir?" },
    { "sp": "Reporter", "t": "How many were there, sir?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Oh, excuse me! Isn't the staff meeting at 9:30? You're in the wrong conference room. It's up one floor." },
    { "sp": "Managing Partner", "t": "This is a major class action. A case that could propel us to the top rank of full service firms." },
    { "sp": "Managing Partner", "t": "Anyway, Sheffrin-Marks fired their last firm because they took their eye off the ball. Until further notice, your personal lives have been cancelled." },
    { "sp": "Managing Partner", "t": "And Alicia will take the pro bono." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Will. Alicia. Hey. I'm sorry I didn't introduce you in there. Everything's moving real fast with this class action." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Don't be nervous. You worried about the gunshot residue?" },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "So, Will speaks highly of you. He says you graduated top of your class at Georgetown. When was this?" },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "I want you to think of me as a mentor, Alicia. It's the closest thing we have to an old boys' network in this town. Women helping women, okay?" },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "When I was starting out, I got one great piece of advice. Men can be lazy. Women can't. And I think that goes double for you." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Jennifer Lewis. 26 years old. Taught second grade. Accused of killing her ex-husband. The prosecution thought it was a slam dunk 45 years, but the jury came back last week deadlocked." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Six jurors voted to convict, six not. I'm not even sure why the State Attorney is re-trying except he wants Justice! He wants to prove himself." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "The police focused on Jennifer so early in the investigation, they never even looked for the carjacker. Deadlock a jury a second time, they'll never re-try a third." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Okay." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "Kalinda Sharma. I'm the in-house. The investigator. You're Peter Florrick's wife." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "Gunshot residue was found on Jennifer's face and hands. That's why the cops started to think it was a murder disguised as a carjacking." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "According to them, Jennifer shot her ex-husband and wiped down the gun." },
    { "sp": "Court Clerk", "t": "All rise. The Criminal Court of Cook County is now in session, the Honorable Judge Richard Cuesta presiding. Be seated." },
    { "sp": "Judge Cuesta", "t": "The judge reaches into his pocket, pulls out a check, handing it to the plaintiff. 'I'm returning five grand and we're going to decide this.'" },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "Your Honor, just to refresh your memory, the accused was deemed a flight risk due to an earlier custody hearing in which she threatened to run off with her daughter." },
    { "sp": "Judge Cuesta", "t": "And yet just last week a jury deadlocked on these murder charges six to six, Mr. Brody. Now I know our new state's attorney wants to look tough, but why are you fighting this?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Yes, Your Honor. Your husband and I never quite saw eye to eye, ma'am." },
    { "sp": "Judge Cuesta", "t": "But if the prosecution thinks that this will in some way prejudice me against your client, he is sorely mistaken. Nice try, Matan. So Ms. Lewis is granted pretrial release with electronic monitoring." },
    { "sp": "Jennifer Lewis", "t": "We had an amazing time. Michael talked about missing his daughter, the life we had together. We were driving home and, uh, we got a flat." },
    { "sp": "Jennifer Lewis", "t": "He was getting out to fix it, and I saw a red pickup truck. A man came up to Michael. I didn't see the gun until Michael! And I saw Michael's face." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "You say this man ran to a red pickup truck here, and took off in this direction across the parking lot. There's a surveillance camera here. And the prosecution played it to make you look like a liar." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "But there's no truck on the tape. And that's why you're facing a retrial now and not an acquittal." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I think what Kalinda is trying to say, Jennifer is, is it possible that you were mistaken and that the car went this way not that way?" },
    { "sp": "Dr. Foreman", "t": "That's right. I was the jury foreman. In fact, I'm not even sure why the other side went to trial. I voted for conviction right from the start." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "But I'm with the defense. Can I ask you how many voted for conviction from the start, sir?" },
    { "sp": "Dr. Foreman", "t": "Eleven. There was only one holdout. Juror number nine. We argued with her for three days." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Foreman", "t": "The judge would only declare us deadlocked if we were evenly split. So some of us agreed to change our vote to not guilty just to get out of there." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Duretsky", "t": "They all thought they were so much smarter than me. My vote counted just as much as theirs. I tend to look at a person and size them up pretty quickly. That lady, I liked her." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "The one in the Brioni? He said to me, 'May the best man win.' So this is a contest between me and Cary?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "So this is a contest between me and Cary? It was either that or a cage match." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "I need you to sign some things. We used most of it for your court costs. The rent will come out of my salary." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Alicia, I know this has been hard on you, but you have to believe me. I'm innocent of the abuse of office charges." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "They were playing a tape in Grace's computer lab of you sucking the toes of a hooker. You think I care about the small print in your employment contract?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "There was a rumor going around that something got buried, pitted. Evidence or testimony." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "As soon as the cops found gunshot residue on Jennifer's hands, they had their suspect. Page one of the crime lab summary. Look at the top corner. It is a staple. And that's odd because there's no page two." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "Why do they need to staple if there's no page two? Either they kept something out of the discovery that pointed to another suspect, or it would be helpful in court to imply that they did." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Two days away from trial and you're thinking of dropping the old strategy? Diane barely convinced a cat lady to acquit." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "So, a pickup truck is seen racing through a well-lit, empty parking lot. How is a truck missed by that surveillance camera? Mismarked surveillance tape." },
    { "sp": "Cindy Lewis", "t": "And how would you describe your husband's relationship with his first wife? Well, most of the time it was fine, friendly even, but after the last custody hearing, Michael was pretty worried about Jennifer." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And you were married to the victim for how long, Mrs. Lewis? Thank you. Your witness." },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "Objection, Your Honor. Hearsay. Now, Mrs. Lewis, you stated that Michael was worried about Jennifer, yet in the week following—" },
    { "sp": "Judge Cuesta", "t": "I'll allow it. Go ahead, Mrs. Lewis." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Now, Detective Briggs, the defense has referred to three unsolved carjackings in the neighborhood at that time. Are you aware of this?" },
    { "sp": "Detective Briggs", "t": "They were all luxury cars: one Lexus and two BMWs. The victim in this crime was driving a 2001 Honda, and there was no clip on the fuel line. That's what the carjackers did to force the luxury cars over." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Detective Briggs, what is 'the pit'? In police circles, what is 'the pit'?" },
    { "sp": "Detective Briggs", "t": "The pit is police slang for evidence thought irrelevant at a crime scene. When an officer's referring to dropping something in 'the pit,' or 'pitting it,' he refers to excluding it from the crime scene narrative." },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "Your Honor, objection! Evidence is logged in all the time that proves irrelevant to the discovery. It's not an intent to deceive. It's an attempt to decipher facts." },
    { "sp": "Judge Cuesta", "t": "Okay, okay, Mrs. Florrick! None of this was in the first trial. Is it your intention to pursue a new defense? Did you bury something?" },
    { "sp": "Judge Cuesta", "t": "I'll give you till Monday to produce them along with any evidence they reference. Then I'll rule on admissibility. And you, Mrs. Florrick, I'm not sure if you're being fed this stuff or you're doing it on your own, but if it's the former, you're walking a very narrow ethical line here. You understand that?" },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "It's dog hair. The pitted evidence. Cops pitted it because they found dog hair at Michael's residence and thought it was the same. It's from an Italian greyhound. Neither Michael nor Jennifer had greyhounds." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "So we're looking for an arthritic greyhound owner." },
    { "sp": "Mr. North", "t": "The computer automatically records the surveillance. Marks it with date and time. I mean, even if the computer did mismark it, I make an hourly tour of the lot, and I didn't see no pickup truck." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "This is at 11:03 the night of the murder, Mr. North, and that's you making your hourly circuit of the lot, correct? And you saw no pickup truck, no carjacker racing past, nothing the defendant claims she saw." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "It's a plastic shopping bag. It was a very windy night that night, and it blew across the lot at 11:48. So let's fast-forward the other two monitors—the night before the murder and the night after the murder to the same time code. What do you see?" },
    { "sp": "Mr. North", "t": "No, it it's not wh what it looks like. It gets cold out there, and sometimes you don't make the circuit of the lot. So on the nights that you don't go out, you don't record the actual surveillance image, you set your computer up to duplicate the night before." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Just so I'm clear, there is no recording the night of the murder, and you were never there to see or not see the pickup truck or the carjacker. No further questions." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "So, you're wondering whether demolishing the key prosecution witness didn't just save your ass as first chair—nice work, by the way—but was enough for reasonable doubt. Nail in the tire. And the old Honda. It just doesn't feel like a carjacking. It feels like a murder made to look like a carjacking." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "I don't think we've ever met before. I'm Glenn Childs. You know he's using you, don't you? Peter blames me for his downfall. He's using you to get to me." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Mr. Childs, the day you leaked that sex tape to the press and forced me to shield my children from every cable news station that played it in a 24-hour rotation, that was the day I became collateral damage." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Mrs. Lewis, did you and the deceased sign a prenuptial agreement? So if the deceased were to divorce you, let's say, in order to reunite with his first wife, you would be cut off from his premarital savings, is that correct?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "The police never questioned your brother, isn't that correct, Mrs. Lewis? Isn't it a fact that a year ago at the time of the murder, your brother worked at a dog track?" },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "Jury's in? Well, Mrs. Florrick, Chicago Homicide has decided to reopen its investigation into the murder of Michael Lewis. The dog hairs admitted into evidence match those found at his workplace. So our state's attorney has decided to withdraw the charges against your client and pursue a case against Mrs. Lewis's brother." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Yes. Thank you. Thank you so much." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "You did great. Oh, one more thing. You've been made my second chair in the civil case. I'll see you tomorrow at 9:30, staff meeting." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Hey, it's me. I'm sorry, you must be in bed, but I just wanted to tell you, the appellate court agreed to hear my case. I still can't believe it. I love you." }
  ],
  "S01E02": [
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "If you want me to forgive you, I need to know what I'm forgiving you for." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "So 3,000$ a pop, that's what 60,000$? Did you pay her not to use a condom? Was that extra? Your trip to Houston, did you take her with you?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "You know I didn't." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I don't know what I know. Grace's 12th birthday party, you s You said you had to leave early. Where did you go, Peter? You answer those questions, and then we will talk about forgiveness." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "This is a pretrial hearing, so don't worry about the driver yet. We just need to win a few motions." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Sorry! Don't worry about it." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "It all comes down to the rape kit. Still no luck finding the other stripper. All we have is a first name, 'Maura,' and a general description. We need to box McKeon in." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "There's only one of two ways his semen ends up in that rape kit: consensual sex or rape. We get him to swear there was no consensual sex. Why would he do that? Because he has a wife." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Kalinda, I need you to get upstairs, get in the state's attorney's office and wait for the subpoena." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "I'll do that. You all right? Yeah, sure, I'm fine." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Oh, my God, this is too good. We got Judge Abernathy. He's our newest judge. He's so liberal, he makes Michael Moore look like Rush Limbaugh. Justice may be blind, but judges sure aren't." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "All rise for Judge Abernathy. Oh, please, don't stand. Everybody sit back down, please. We've got the big room here, don't we?" },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "Your Honor, given the stature of my client, Mr. McKeon, and given the fact that this pretrial hearing has already garnered the attention of our friends in the press, We would ask the court to seal the pretrial filings and avoid a show trial." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Mr. Ericcson, I don't think we need to do all that. Do we? First amendment issues and all? I deny the petition with regret. Mr. Gardner?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Yes, Your Honor. We have a lot of testimony focusing on whether there was a consensual act between Mr. McKeon and my client, but if Mr. McKeon is willing to stipulate there was, indeed, a consensual sexual act, we would forego this testimony." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "That's a good point. Mr. Ericcson, how do you respond?" },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "We will stipulate there was no sex of any kind, forced or consensual, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "The plaintiff also requests an expedited trial date, Your Honor, a DNA sample from Mr. McKeon, DNA results from the rape kit, and the investigative reports from the state's attorney's office. They have been reluctant to furnish them." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Thank you, counselor. I will grant all four motions. My docket is clear. Five days? How's that for expedited? As a side note, I would like to ask for a moment of silence for the recent mass killings in Darfur. Thank you. Court adjourned." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "So I find this odd, Mr. Grant. You were there, and you say there was no rape? So either you're lying or my client's lying." },
    { "sp": "Grant", "t": "All I know is what I saw." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Mr. Grant, this is a copy of your wife's w-2. Could you read the name of your wife's employer, please?" },
    { "sp": "Grant", "t": "TZK Industries." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "And TZK Industries is a company owned by the McKeon family to supply furniture to their properties." },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "Objection. Not in evidence." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "I think it's all right for it to be asked, Mr. Ericcson. I actually, sustained. I did. Mr. Ericcson had a point." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Isn't it an amazing coincidence, Mr. Grant, that after a year of unemployment, your wife got a job in June from the McKeon family, a month after the bachelor party incident? And why is that, Mr. Grant?" },
    { "sp": "Grant", "t": "Because my wife and I are divorced. My wife and I have been separated for a year now, so her getting this job has nothing to do with me." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "But, sir, wouldn't you benefit even if only in the form of lowered spousal support? No further questions, Your Honor. I'm sorry. I didn't see it. Don't ever let your first chair get undercut like that again." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I just got the rape kit results. State's attorney wasn't stonewalling. They sent it out to a commercial lab and just got it back. It's not McKeon's DNA." },
    { "sp": "Christy", "t": "I'm not lying. I It's really not his sperm? You need to find Maura." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "We can't find Maura. You really don't remember anything to help us find her? She was on her cell phone the whole night till her battery ran out." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Manny Lyons - who never set foot in Chicago - isn't it true, Dr. Girtzman, the only connection between Manny Lyons and Christy barbosa is that you worked on both cases?" },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Mrs. Florrick, this is not a debating society. Sustained." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Dr. Girtzman, would you say that the standard of a commercial lab is relaxed? And yet isn't it true, that your lab almost lost its accreditation in 2005 when it cross-contaminated two DNA samples that were out-sourced?" },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "Your Honor, given this testimony, we move that the rape kit be reexamined at a second genetic lab." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "I think that is an understandable request, Mr. Ericcson." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Your Honor, we would stipulate that Mrs. Florrick has proven her case. Dr. Girtzman cross-contaminated the sample." },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "But we would argue, therefore, that is the very reason it cannot be tested again. The very fact that it is agreed that the sample is now contaminated means it can't possibly be tested with any authority." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Mr. Gardner, you argued that the rape kit was contaminated. You can't now argue it's probative. The plaintiff's motion for retesting is denied. Motion is denied." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Actually, we have a strange request. We need to get a printout of a phone bill from four months ago. McKeon. Lloyd. Hard copy." },
    { "sp": "Rosemary", "t": "There was a bachelor party here in May that resulted in a a rape. Were you here then? I'll have to speak to a manager." },
    { "sp": "Rosemary", "t": "Christy and Maura arrived at 10:15. Right there, 11:20. Out-of-state area code." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Were you the other dancer there that night, Maura? And your driver, Mr. Grant, what did he tell you after you took Christy to the hospital? And that's why you went back home to Michigan? And to reiterate, Christy told you about the rape immediately afterward?" },
    { "sp": "Maura", "t": "I was, yes. He told us that the McKeons were very rich, and we should all keep our mouths shut. Yes. I'm sorry. I was scared. Yes. In fact, he followed her out into the parking lot and he said, 'don't tell anyone. Just take the money and shut up.'" },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "All rise for Judge Abernathy. Good afternoon. I have given this case quite a bit of thought, as you can imagine. You have both argued your case well. But I find myself judging in favor of the defendant." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Without a DNA match to the rape kit, we have a classic he-said, she-said; and as much as my personal sympathies lie with the plaintiff, the evidence does not warrant a favorable decision. Judgment in favor of the defense." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Are you okay? I'm sorry. I believe you." },
    { "sp": "McKeon", "t": "This is all I want to say. I have been falsely accused by a woman who wanted nothing more than a quick payday. But every year there are thousands of women who are abused, and I don't think we should let this false accusation—" },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Mr. McKeon. You're under arrest for the rape of Christy Barbosa." }
  ],
  "S01E03": [
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "And hour ago I resigned As state's attorney of cook county. I want to be clear I have never abused my office." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Just wanted to say thank you for the opportunity. It's a real lifesaver." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "No, glad you could come aboard. Not only are you coming back to the workplace fairly late, but you have some very prominent baggage. But, hey, if she can do it, so can you." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "Kalinda Sharma. The investigator. You're Peter Florrick's wife. She's a junior associate who doesn't think she's a junior associate. Her husband was the states attorney. She lived in Highland park." },
    { "sp": "Zach Florrick", "t": "Dad told us he made mistakes. Yeah, but not that." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Come on, guys, 15 minutes. Grace, come on, you got to eat. And soccer tryouts are today." },
    { "sp": "Grace Florrick", "t": "Mom, I hate this school. Why would I want to play for it?" },
    { "sp": "Automated Voice", "t": "This is a collect call from tamms correctional institution from Peter Florrick. To decline this call press one. To accept this call press two." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Are you all right? Hanging in there. The kids there? Can I talk to them? I'll let you go. Tell them I called." },
    { "sp": "Kenny Chatham", "t": "Mrs. Florrick. It's been awhile, huh? I think I need a lawyer. Last night, me and this guy brian We went to my buddy's house to get, uh, we were just looking to party." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "To get what? Weed or whatever. Look, I'm not sure I understand what happened, But if you were buying pot—You need to go to the police. But I'd still need a lawyer, right?" },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "Don't move. And get rid of that. Can you find out if there was any police activity in Highland park last night? And see if kenny chatham is a person of interest or if there're any warrants on him." },
    { "sp": "Lauren Chatham", "t": "What are you What are you doing? You look Great. I've been meaning to call I'm sorry to just show up like this, but I need to talk to you about Kenny. Where is he? My car. Asked me to smooth the waters." },
    { "sp": "Police Officer", "t": "What's he being charged with? Felony murder." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "They're saying kenny did this? Yep. Along with brian keller. The police allege that these two broke into a guest apartment owned by the rankin family. Because the guard was killed in the commission of an alleged burglary, It's felony murder." },
    { "sp": "Kenny Chatham", "t": "I didn't know anybody died, I don't know how anybody could have. I told you, I ran home. I yelled up at Brian to get out, but he couldn't hear me, he was still inside. I never even saw the guard, just his car!" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Then why did you lie to me, Kenny? Why didn't you tell me you broke in? Okay. There'll be a bail hearing, and a judge will decide if you can be released pending trial. Talk to spencer. He's my friend. He knew we were coming." },
    { "sp": "Richard Chatham", "t": "Richard. You want to explain to me why the attorney I hired For my son just got turned away? Without our consent. He's 18." },
    { "sp": "Terry Streiger", "t": "Terry streiger. And excuse me for saying so, mrs Florrick, But I think you're in over your head. Terry's been a defense attorney For more than 20 years. He's a top-rated aba member, He's well-known and well-respected." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Thank you for your time, but we'll take it from here. He doesn't need your consent, he's 18. And although I might not be as seasoned as mr. Streiger is, I can guarantee you I care a lot more about your son than he does. Kenny will get the best defense." },
    { "sp": "Judge Hovick", "t": "Court is now in session. The honorable earl hovick presiding. Ms. Raines." },
    { "sp": "Ms. Raines", "t": "Your honor, Due to the seriousness of the charges, The state opposes bail at this time. We believe the defendant Not only poses a flight risk but a danger to the community." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Excuse me, your honor, but based on what? Mr. Chatham has no criminal history, Close ties to the community. The state has secured A cooperating witness, co-defendant brian keller. Who not only places mr. Chatham At the crime scene But identifies him as the attacker." },
    { "sp": "Judge Hovick", "t": "Then bail is denied. Defendant is remanded to custody. Next case." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Your friend brian must've cut a deal with the state's attorney. The first one who does is usually the first one out. Let's go." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "We didn't get bail because brian cut a deal with the s. A. He got immunity on felony murder By taking a lesser charge of burglary And agreeing to testify against kenny. So what we need to do is establish there was no burglary." },
    { "sp": "Spencer Rankin", "t": "You guys are cops? No, we're just trying to help your friends. I don't know anything about what happened." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "They're not gonna happen anymore though, are they? Because the cops are saying kenny broke into your place. And you know why they're saying that? Because they don't get how good of a friend you are to him. They don't get what you and he share. They don't get you told him it was okay to help himself to your weed that night. Yeah, and because of that the ken-ster's facing life in prison. So, what do you need me to do?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "We tell the S. A. We got a witness corroborating permission And we cut kenny's jail time by 10 years. Without the burglary charge, he can't be held accountable For what happened after he left a crime scene." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "Kenny has no alibi. His parents were asleep when he got home. So this brian, he's got a temper. Guidance counselor says he's been suspended twice for fighting. Kenny, however, was always seen as a sweet kid Till he started hanging out with brian, that is. Pretty toxic friendship." },
    { "sp": "Shelly", "t": "Everyone's pretty shaken up. I mean, this sort of thing just doesn't happen around here. Scandal maybe, but murder? I work second shift, Not that I ever get out on time. My relief's always late." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And, shelly, when you were leaving, do you remember seeing anyone on the street? Anyone running? I mean I think I saw someone go inside across the way. Because they turned the yard lights off right after made it hard to fill out my timecard." },
    { "sp": "Ms. Raines", "t": "You have no physical evidence linking my client to the victim. No DNA, nothing. Even so, You can't prove he wasn't an accomplice to the burglary. And if someone dies during the commission, it's felony murder. Regardless of who killed him. We'll do involuntary manslaughter." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Manslaughter, since Burglary's not in play anymore. What? Did I forget to mention that we can prove kenny had permission to enter? Spencer rankin's willing to testify. Make it two and we got a deal." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Is that even possible? Run that distance in five minutes? Only one way to find out. The average teenage boy sprinting would likely be In the range of eight to ten miles per hour. Okay, it's two minutes and we're— Ass! Nice." },
    { "sp": "Richard Chatham", "t": "You come Within 100 feet of my son again, And I swear, I'll sue you, The chathams, and your entire firm. Spencer's got nothing to do with this and how dare you Intimidate him into giving false information. There was never any permission given to enter, There was never any pot and he is never going to testify!" },
    { "sp": "Judge Hovick", "t": "This is a pre-trial hearing. Which is another way of saying both sides should come to an agreement Before we go any farther. Be advised there is no jury in this proceeding, there's just me." },
    { "sp": "Ms. Raines", "t": "At 11:24 p. M. , Brian was pulled over for speeding, Approximately four blocks from the rankin house. And officer diaz, did brian give any statement At that time? So, in effect, he placed himself at the crime scene." },
    { "sp": "Officer Diaz", "t": "Yes, he did. He immediately confessed to having fled The scene of a burglary on belson street. And was arrested at that time." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Officer diaz, Mr. Keller had this 'spontaneous utterance', Did he mention that anyone had been killed? So, when brian told you that Kenny chatham was responsible for the victim's death, He had had time to concoct a story. Nothing further." },
    { "sp": "Brian Keller", "t": "Kenny wanted to get high and he said he knew where he could get some weed. He never said anything about breaking in, which is why I was surprised when he asked me to help jam the window open. So I went downstairs. To wait for him so we could get the heck out of there. But then I saw the security guard." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "According to patrol records, he called in a perimeter check at 11:15. Do you know what time it was when you saw the guard? So, 11:18? And how long were you there after you saw the guard? Couple minutes. Which puts us at 11:20." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Were you wet? The police report made no mention Of your being wet at the time of your arrest. But were you? I'd like to introduce the bylaws of the Highland Park Neighborhood association Into evidence. Their automatic sprinkler system Is set to go off every night at 11:15 And run for 15 minutes. If you had been where you said you were, You would have gotten soaked. The truth is, you weren't standing behind the bush. You were standing over the guard. Nothing further." },
    { "sp": "Judge Hovick", "t": "That you pursue a charge of this magnitude Without a thorough investigation Is unconscionable. No direct or circumstantial evidence linking the defendant to the murder, No eyewitness testimony other than that of a co-defendant who has a vested interest in the outcome of the trial. Ms. Raines, I strongly suggest You find a way to work with the defense counsel To find a more equitable solution." },
    { "sp": "Ms. Raines", "t": "Breaking and entering, Plus possession of marijuana." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Trespassing and possession. Look, You're not guaranteed of a manslaughter conviction against Brian. If you can't spin that into a win with the brass, You're in the wrong line of work." },
    { "sp": "Lauren Chatham", "t": "No jail time. Thank you. Thank you so much. Kenny just has to perform 1,000 hours of community service. And participate in mandatory drug counseling." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "First plea bargain, a gin martini. It's another stern, lockhart tradition." }
  ],
  "S01E04": [
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "My clients manufactured Zennapril, and stop poking at them like perps on 'America's most wanted'. I didn't hear an objection. Let's just refrain from the crosstalk. Yes, Your Honor. I will try not to interrupt Miss Lockhart again. It'll be difficult, but I'll try." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "So Ray, How would you describe your physical condition now? You're looking at it. Mostly it's been hard on Carol. We got married a year ago, before my stroke, and she didn't buy into this." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Brandon, Lahna, you take point on legal precedent. Cary, take Alicia and review anything that could undercut the class action. I don't want to win the battle and lose the war. Use Kalinda if you need to, okay? Let's get to it." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Mrs. Demory. Can I get you something? I'm fine. Sorry, must have had a zombie look on my face. We all need a moment. I'm Alicia. Right, you replaced Bree. Nice to meet you." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Demory", "t": "This is what keeps us going. Michael Graff. He took Zennapril after a little league game. Jenny Hollis. She was a dancer. They all wrote you? Yes. Just they're all counting on us, you know? If we don't win, they're stuck." },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "Objection. Relevance. Isn't it the case, the only reason you're not suing your doctor is because his pockets aren't as deep as my clients's? Objection! Please, Your Honor. Approach the bench." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Get me all references to secondary prescriptions in Ray's deposition. I need you to look into this. What are you saying? Someone's bribing a juror? I'm not saying anything. I just found it. This could be anything." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "They're the Wolks. She's a hairdresser. And he's actually, I think he's out of work. Guess they came into some money. Hey, there he is now. That's the husband Derek. Oh, gosh! I'm sorry. I forgot I have to meet the realtor. Thanks so much for your time." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Objection, Your Honor. Is this really necessary? The Demorys are seeking damages for loss of consortium, Your Honor. The jury needs to understand that loss. Counsel, the term 'consortium,' is not limited. I know what consortium means. Please, I have prided myself in not using my gavel once in my 30 years on the bench. What's going on? What? I just overruled. Ka-ching." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Demory", "t": "Before your husband's stroke, how often did you have sexual relations? I don't know I guess twice a week. And since the stroke, how often do you have sex? Would you say it was less? Yes. Less. But your husband isn't completely paralyzed from the waist down, is he? No." },
    { "sp": "Daniel", "t": "It's Daniel. Peter's lawyer. Do you have a minute? I was wondering if you had look for the Kozko letters. We're struggling with these weekend meetings that he had with Peter, last March. The ninth, the 16th and the 25th." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "So, I tracked down Tober's ex-wife. She says he was five months behind on alimony. Then last week, he paid her ten grand. He claims he took out a second mortgage on his house. Tober doesn't own a house. Kalinda, do you know someone named Kozko? Gerald Kozko? The real estate developer." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "If his payoff's $35,000, then McCloon owes him another $15,000. My guess is he gets it before deliberations. That's at the end of today. The jury's already deliberating. They give their verdict today. So if we pull the trigger, we do it now." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Winsor", "t": "Then let me continue quoting: 'primary outcome for Zennapril include a 40% reduction in the average frequencies of migraines.' We test drugs for all possible outcomes. It doesn't mean that we recommend them. Could you please tell the court What this is, doctor? Discovery materials, item 469." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "This document is an application to the emea, seeking approval to market Zennapril in Europe as a migraine medication. Am I correct? Yes. Filed 20 months ago. Dr. Winsor, would you please tell the court whose signature is on this application? My signature. No further questions, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "And she's sure it was a payoff? She saw an envelope exchange hands, but she doesn't know what was inside the envelope. And she didn't see the driver? She saw a dark green SUV. Kalinda saw a partial plate j15. She's running it now, crossing it with make and model." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "The judge wants you in chambers. This should be interesting. In the next minutes, we're going to figure something out. I would like to request a private meeting. In the meantime, I would like silence. Not McCloon silence, not Lockhart silence, Just silence!!! Unless I point to you, You will not speak." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "The jury has reached a verdict. I'm holding that verdict until we settle an issue that has arisen. The foreman has approached me about being followed by someone from one of your legal teams. Who would that be? You may speak. Your honor, we have reason to believe that Mr. McCloon bribed one of the jurors." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Mr. McCloon, without the outrage, please. Clearly, Miss Lockhart has nothing solid, and she's trying to torpedo this verdict before it comes in. All right, Miss Lockhart, I am holding you in contempt. You owe the legal defense fund $80,000 to be paid no later than Friday of next week. And I think I am damn well owed an apology." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Now we are all going back into that courtroom, and we are all living with this verdict. If you have evidence of jury tampering, Miss Lockhart, take it up on appeal. Ladies and gentleman of the jury, have you reached a verdict? Yes, Your Honor. And what is your verdict?" },
    { "sp": "Jury Foreman", "t": "'We, the jury, find for Raymond Demory and against the defendant Zennapril pharmaceutical. And we assess damages in the sum of $800,000 in compensatory damages and three million in punitive damages.' Thank you for your service to the state of Illinois. The jury is dismissed. Court adjourned." },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "Settle, and I won't pursue an appeal. I still have my class action clients. Right. I'll get you some numbers by the end of the day. So, we were wrong? It happens. People sometimes just look guilty. Thank you. It was It was all Diane. I want you to have something. These are the people that you helped today." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "I will try not to interrupt Miss Lockhart again. It'll be difficult, but I'll try. We just need to win a few motions." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Brandon, Lahna, you take point on legal precedent. Cary, take Alicia and review anything that could undercut the class action. I don't want to win the battle and lose the war. Use Kalinda if you need to, okay? Let's get to it." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "I just need one thing. And why do I help you, again? Because you like me. That still works with people? Like a charm. You never should have quit this place. I didn't quit. I was fired." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "You're telling me your husband had nothing to do with this? Mr. Childs, my law firm is bringing on a civil action, that's all. Designed to embarrass my office and make it look like I don't care about a rape victim. I have kids, too. And you should know I was holding back. I had more to release about your husband, a lot more." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Objection, Your Honor! This is a research document, not promotional material." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "The place mat, the $35,000, the alimony. You request another juror now, it'd be a definite mistrial. But I feel like the jury was with me. I mean, the summation they were nodding." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Sergeant Largon! Sergeant, come in, please. And wait right there. Anyone who speaks without my permission will be held in contempt. Now, the jury has reached a verdict. I'm holding that verdict until we settle an issue that has arisen." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Your honor, we have reason to believe that Mr. McCloon bribed one of the jurors. We intended to bring these charges to Your Honor, but first, we wanted to be certain of our information." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Mr. McCloon, without the outrage, please. Clearly, Miss Lockhart has nothing solid, and she's trying to torpedo this verdict before it comes in. All right, Miss Lockhart, I am holding you in contempt. You owe the legal defense fund $80,000 to be paid no later than Friday of next week." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "I did not point to you. That'll be $10,000, Mr. McCloon. Now we are all going back into that courtroom, and we are all living with this verdict. If you have evidence of jury tampering, Miss Lockhart, take it up on appeal. Too many people have selflessly invested their time for your playground tactics to throw this into a mistrial." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Thanks again. It really is amazing. Ever been to the hungry kitten? The restaurant? Sure. Ray loves it. And you borrowed our deposition books? Yes. Why? Diane wanted us to look through ray's earlier statements. You didn't have to do it. You would have won. We did win." }
  ],
  "S01E05": [
    { "sp": "Jackie Florrick", "t": "Congratulations. That's great. Your husband's appeal. It looks like he could get out of prison any month now. Early Show had a story on it. Some good stuff on you, too. Did you catch it?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "No, missed it. So litigation wants me on this new class action. That's where I'm headed. When did you say this was?" },
    { "sp": "Christy", "t": "May 15, four months ago. Had you ever met Mr. McKeon before? No. I'd seen his family on the news. I heard about their hotels and stuff, but that's about it." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Well, the problem here, Christy, is that any civil suit could be seen as opportunistic. Because of their money? Yes. I'm sorry, but I didn't choose my rapist. This kid doesn't know what she's in for. Taking on the McKeon family." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "It's a criminal case, not a civil one. Except the new state's attorney rejected the charges. Because it's got Duke Lacrosse written all over it. Put your politics aside, Diane. You're Glenn Childs, you get this dropped in your lap a stripper at a bachelor party accusing the son of the most powerful family in Chicago of rape." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "210. We need to keep our eye on the ball. And Christy is just gonna look like she's trying to make some quick cash from the McKeon family." },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "Ericcson here. Will. I think you know why I'm calling. The only reason someone's forced out of public office in America these days is sex. So let's talk about Lloyd McKeon. It's so sad, really. I remember when you had a respectable firm, Will." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "And what charges might those be, Will? Do you think you're the first firm she's shopping this lawsuit to? There's nothing there. Even the state's attorney the new state's attorney thinks there's nothing there. And yet I got you to a settlement lunch on a half hour's notice." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Mrs. Florrick, what would you say to someone bringing unsubstantiated sexual charges against a public figure just in order to gain an advantage? I would say you're trying to change the subject from rape." },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "We're willing to make an offer. It's nonnegotiable. You have till end of business day. McKeon did it. Under a hundred thou, I would've said no. Over a hundred thou What about well over?" },
    { "sp": "Christy", "t": "We don't have to go to court? He just offered that? What? Can't we tell the police? It's standard in civil agreements. He'll know how much it costs to rape somebody and get away with it, that's all. This is about him admitting that he did this to me." },
    { "sp": "Christy", "t": "12 clients, stockbroker types, no one out of control. They paid $400 for a 90-minute, two-girl fantasy strip. Who was the other girl? Maura I don't remember her last name. That was the only time she worked." },
    { "sp": "Christy", "t": "The groom? He offered me another $100 for a private lap dance. When I got in the bedroom, he locked the door. Then he grabbed me. I tried to get the mace from my bag, tried to shout. Did you tell anyone afterwards? Maura. And our driver Grant." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Didn't you think that might be important to tell us? I haven't gone on a call in a year. I was in an abusive relationship. I needed the money to move out. But still stripped. A call girl can't get raped? That's my point, thanks." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "This is settlement poker. No one wants to go to trial, but the person who plays the better hand makes the better deal. So get me some good cards. Cary, right? Yes, sir. Nice suit. I got a job for you. I need you to take Alicia's sheffrin-marks depos for the week." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "And why do I help you, again? You never should have quit this place. You ran a solid rape investigation. You should be dying to slip us the witness interviews. I had more to release about your husband, a lot more. Disturbing things, but out of compassion, I held back." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "We just need contact information. That's all, Ms. Avarski, for the driver and the stripper. The day rate is $31,000, dawn to dawn. So about this rape. Can you get me contact information on this dancer? Maura? She quit right afterwards. Left a fake number, fake address, fake social." },
    { "sp": "Grant", "t": "Which was? Christy gave the guy a lap dance, he paid her, we went home. Nobody said anything about any rape. I don't blame Christy for trying to get extra money out of McKeon, but, hey, she's lying." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "So two months in, you seem well. Some good guys here. There's probably less backstabbing than the state attorney's office. So, here's the thing about Childs. Everything he does is a political calculation. I didn't tell your mom. Daniel told me." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Your appellate lawyer? He's the one who referred Christy to you. So he what? Well I mentioned to Daniel that you were with Stern, Lockhart, and that if he didn't have time for Christy's case that maybe you would. You had sex with her. What? You're using her first name, Peter. She's one of your whores." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "This is a pretrial hearing, so don't worry about the driver yet. We just need to win a few motions. It all comes down to the rape kit. Still no luck finding the other stripper. All we have is a first name, 'Maura,' and a general description. We need to box McKeon in." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "All rise for Judge Abernathy. Oh, please, don't stand. Everybody sit back down, please. We've got the big room here, don't we? Mr. Ericcson, I don't think we need to do all that. Do we? First amendment issues and all? I deny the petition with regret. Mr. Gardner?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Yes, Your Honor. We have a lot of testimony focusing on whether there was a consensual act between Mr. McKeon and my client, but if Mr. McKeon is willing to stipulate there was, indeed, a consensual sexual act, we would forego this testimony." },
    { "sp": "William Ericcson", "t": "We will stipulate there was no sex of any kind, forced or consensual, Your Honor. The plaintiff also requests an expedited trial date, Your Honor, a DNA sample from Mr. McKeon, DNA results from the rape kit, and the investigative reports from the state's attorney's office." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "I will grant all four motions. My docket is clear. Five days? How's that for expedited? As a side note, I would like to ask for a moment of silence for the recent mass killings in Darfur. Thank you. Court adjourned." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Mr. Grant, this is a copy of your wife's w-2. Could you read the name of your wife's employer, please? And TZK Industries is a company owned by the McKeon family to supply furniture to their properties. Isn't it an amazing coincidence, Mr. Grant, that after a year of unemployment, your wife got a job in June from the McKeon family, a month after the bachelor party incident?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I just got the rape kit results. State's attorney wasn't stonewalling. They sent it out to a commercial lab and just got it back. It's not McKeon's DNA. Grant says you're lying. McKeon says you're lying. We need to get the full who else did you have sex with that night? No one. I swear." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "The rape kit came back. It's not his sperm. It's not McKeon's DNA. We can't find Maura. You really don't remember anything to help us find her? She was on her cell phone the whole night till her battery ran out." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Girtzman", "t": "Doctor. And these are your initials, 'gg' right at the bottom of the report? Yes, those are mine. This isn't 5795. Yes, this is 5794. The Manny Lyons case. My mistake. I see that you ran the analysis on both cases. Given the sequential numbering, I imagine that you worked on one right after the other?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "In both cases, the DNA matched the same culprit, Manny Lyons, is that correct? Manny Lyons - who never set foot in Chicago - isn't it true, Dr. Girtzman, the only connection between Manny Lyons and Christy barbosa is that you worked on both cases? And yet isn't it true, that your lab almost lost its accreditation in 2005 when it cross-contaminated two DNA samples?" },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Mrs. Florrick, this is not a debating society. Sustained. Mr. Gardner, you argued that the rape kit was contaminated. You can't now argue it's probative. The plaintiff's motion for retesting is denied. Motion is denied." },
    { "sp": "Rosemary", "t": "Christy and Maura arrived at 10:15. Right there, 11:20. Out-of-state area code. I'll have to speak to a manager." },
    { "sp": "Maura", "t": "I was, yes. And your driver, Mr. Grant, what did he tell you after you took Christy to the hospital? He told us that the McKeons were very rich, and we should all keep our mouths shut. Yes. I'm sorry. I was scared. In fact, he followed her out into the parking lot and he said, 'don't tell anyone. Just take the money and shut up.'" },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "All rise for Judge Abernathy. Good afternoon. I have given this case quite a bit of thought, as you can imagine. You have both argued your case well. But I find myself judging in favor of the defendant. Without a DNA match to the rape kit, we have a classic he-said, she-said; and as much as my personal sympathies lie with the plaintiff, the evidence does not warrant a favorable decision. Judgment in favor of the defense." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Are you okay? I'm sorry. I believe you." },
    { "sp": "McKeon", "t": "This is all I want to say. I have been falsely accused by a woman who wanted nothing more than a quick payday. But every year there are thousands of women who are abused, and I don't think we should let this false accusation—" },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Mr. McKeon. You're under arrest for the rape of Christy Barbosa. Cuff him." },
    { "sp": "News Anchor", "t": "Lloyd McKeon was arrested today for rape, literally seconds after he was cleared in a civil case for the identical crime. State's attorney Glenn Childs took credit for finding an untainted sample of DNA that will guarantee McKeon's conviction in a criminal trial." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "I want you to be at this lunch. Don't say anything. Just listen and look serious. How you holding up here? I'm holding up. Oh, yeah, how do you know her? Who? Christy. I don't. Then why did she ask for you to be on the case? I don't know." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I can do that. Christy, I saw you on tv. I thought you'd understand what it's like to be misunderstood." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Ladies. Sorry about that. McKeon's lawyers made a financial offer that Diane and I actually agree on. A first. It's a cash settlement. $450,000. You're kidding. We don't have to go to court? He just offered that?" },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "You're kidding. He just offered that? You'll have to sign a standard confidentiality agreement. Both parties free each other of liability and agree not to speak to anyone, including the press." },
    { "sp": "Christy", "t": "Can't we tell the police? Not if we sign a confidentiality agreement. McKeon could withdraw the award and sue you for defamation. What? It's standard in civil agreements." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Okay, Ericcson, how flexible are you on confidentiality? Exclude new criminal discovery. Come on, this isn't You want to go to court, is that it? Take it as far as pretrial?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And what else did you do there? For a year, I was an escort. Didn't you think that might be important to tell us? A call girl can't get raped? That's my point, thanks." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "The more you bond with these clients, the less helpful you are. Your stripper was hooking. Why is she my stripper when she does something bad? Okay, so the point is to prepare for trial so we don't actually have to go to trial." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "We just need contact information. That's all, Ms. Avarski, for the driver and the stripper. $1,500 an hour, $2,100 and, $3,100. What makes someone more expensive? Regular clients and special requests. No condoms or being dominated or role-playing." },
    { "sp": "Zach Florrick", "t": "Zach so, mom, where are you, anyway? I have to work late tonight. Can you ask grandma if she can stay till 10:00? Grandma, can you stay till 10:00? Mom's working late." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "So two months in, you seem well. Some good guys here. So, here's the thing about Childs. Everything he does is a political calculation. I don't want to be used." }
  ],
  "S01E06": [
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Talk about undue publicity. You say that didn't affect the jury pool? We don't have the time. We have got county clerk. We're late, Clarence Wilcox stays on death row. We can't risk it. Undue publicity argument is the weakest part of the brief. It needs more work." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "There was a cable TV movie broadcasted a week before jury selection. We believe it influenced the jury. We're almost done with the TV movie argument, but we can't specify the saturation of the jury pool. You don't have the ratings broken out? We're not going to make it. Damn." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "People of the State of Illinois v. Clarence Wilcox. Mr. Gardener, we've read your brief, and we're now prepared for oral arguments. You may begin. Mr. Gardener, please explain your undue publicity arguments." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Thank you, Chief Justice. May it please the court. Six years ago, Clarence Wilcox, a 24-year-old Chicago man, was arrested for the murder of a plainclothes officer during the robbery of a grocery store in South Chicago. It's our contention that Clarence Wilcox did not receive a fair trial." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "How is it different from the Scott Peterson appeal? It also involved a TV movie released prior to jury selection. Yes, well, Your Honor, in that case, the TV movie didn't show the Peterson character murdering his victim. In 'Cop Killer', our client is seen shooting the victim. That was a nice last-minute Hail Mary with the TV movie. Judges love novelty in arguments." },
    { "sp": "Patrice Wilcox", "t": "Excuse me, Miss? Sorry to bother you, but my name is Patrice Wilcox. I'm Clarence's wife. Do you know why Clarence wasn't in court today? Because you never said anything about Clarence being innocent. We lost the appeal." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Actual innocence? Yes. We lost the automatic appeal. Now you want to appeal on actual innocence? I read the trial record. Defense never put his wife on the stand. She was his only alibi, and the jury never heard from her. Because wives lie. I can't believe I'm having to tell you this. And she had priors. This guy had his trial, an expensive trial. He killed a cop." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Allegedly. No. found him guilty. That means not allegedly. That means he killed a cop. You know, sometimes people with cute daughters and sweet little wives do bad things. Sometimes very bad things. I'm not helping you on this one. Suit yourself." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Cary wants to take a week on Clarence Wilcox. See if there's any foundation for an evidentiary appeal. On actual innocence. We think it's a good idea for a week. It's good promotable work. And, not to sound too cynical, it burns off the hours we owe the pro bono consortium." },
    { "sp": "Josh Baldwin", "t": "Now, I thought we had a good defense. We could have won. Except it was me against about a half-dozen Asas. This was the first case in that high-profile task force your husband set up. Why didn't you put Patrice on the stand? She had priors. The jury would think she was lying to protect her husband." },
    { "sp": "Tara", "t": "That movie was awful, wasn't it? My dad taped it for me. First of all, I was in there buying cat food. And I was alone. And all of a sudden, I'm this blonde coed who falls in love with a married man. Well, I did see him Wilcox, that was true. He ran through the door, he fired his gun, he yelled at me to kiss the floor, but I peered up, and I saw him kill that police officer." },
    { "sp": "Expert", "t": "That's the six-pack photo array the police present to you. Which one is it? That's him. Yeah, maybe. Hold on. You're confident? You're wrong. The culprit isn't one of these. This is when we explain to the jury about cross-racial identification. Studies still haven't adequately explained why, but it's harder for Caucasians to identify subtle differences in African-American faces." },
    { "sp": "Detective Shores", "t": "Hide everything, quick! Here comes the opposition. That was six years ago. Well, lady comes in, describes the suspect. Male, black, mid-20s, six feet tall, wearing a Bulls sweatshirt. So well, she seemed pretty certain, so we went over to Howie, and he did a sketch. So, we found your guy. He matched the sketch. He had blood spatter on his sweat top." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "The only one in a Bulls sweatshirt. They showed the eyewitness this six-pack before the lineup. I get this to Will. It's enough for an evidentiary appeal. Probably, but if you're going to go all the way with this, you'll have to talk to your husband. He knows where the bodies are buried." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Clarence Wilcox. She's defending a cop killer. But he did it. What I know and what I don't have shifted a bit. This goes back to Childs. He was top man on that case. You know he did it. Here's our problem. You see that? Conversation between family members and convicts is subject to prison surveillance, except under certain circumstances. Conjugal circumstances." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Did they say why? No, but when I tried to take it off, that guy, Shores, he said, 'No. Keep it on.' They wanted it in the picture. Did they want it for evidence? Yeah, but they wanted to take my mug shot first. They made you keep it on for the mug shot?" },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "And, here we are again, Mrs. Florrick. Your Honor? And who do we have over here? Oh, ASA Becker and, my goodness, the deputy state's attorney, Mr. Brody. I thought this case was previously adjudicated, but the appellate court has found reason to reverse my ruling. Not by our hand, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Your Honor, we mean no disrespect. We believe Your Honor was given incorrect information by those trying the case. Objection. So moved. The prosecution rests, Your Honor. Actually, Your Honor, our witnesses are on their way to court right now. Oh, good, well, then we just need a short recess. Ten minutes." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Yeah, that's the sweatshirt he was wearing. And you don't think it's prejudicial to put only one suspect wearing a Bulls sweatshirt in a photo lineup intended to find a suspect in a Bulls sweatshirt? And you found bloodstains on this sweatshirt? He tried to wash the blood out, but it left a stain. And where was the stain, Detective? Here. The witness is pointing to the forearm of his right sleeve." },
    { "sp": "Detective Shores", "t": "So? It's what he was wearing. Yeah, he tried to wash the blood out, but it left a stain. He said he, uh, he was in a pickup game the night before the murder, made contact, and he got a nosebleed. And this blood matched the victim's? No, the police lab could not get a DNA match. Since he washed the shirt, it degraded the blood." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Detective, isn't it true that the only reason you arrested Mr. Wilcox is because he was wearing a Bulls sweatshirt? Detective, do you own a Chicago Bulls sweatshirt? I am trying to show that at the time of Mr. Wilcox's arrest, Bulls apparel was quite popular, so therefore it wouldn't be unusual to find a multitude of suspects wearing Bulls sweatshirts." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Given this testimony, we request that Detective Shores' work product be excluded from the record. Nice try, Mr. Gardener, but there's nothing here that rises to the level of 'fruit of the forbidden tree,' so you'll have to do better than that. But you and Mrs. Florrick do get an 'E' for effort. Any further questions? No, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Brody", "t": "Objection, Your Honor. Detective Shores isn't on the defense's witness list. Is he in court anyway? Maybe we can get something done today. Detective Shores was never found guilty of planting a gun. The judge suppressed the evidence and the attorney declined to prosecute. Is that what Peter was saying?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Excuse me, sir! You can talk to me. You leave my junior associate out of this. Come on, let's face facts here. I think that's what we're trying to do, Mr. Brody. A perjurer? I don't like someone staring me right in the eye and lying to me, and there's not a cop I know who isn't a Bulls fan." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "I'll decide what's irrelevant. If you want to file a complaint, that's why Judy is here. Now, Mr. Gardner, I'm finding now reason to reverse myself. I think you've made a very strong 'fruit of the forbidden tree' argument. If I can't trust Shores, then I can't trust the evidence that's tied to him. So, the line-up, the Bulls sweatshirt, the artist's sketch, they are now stricken from the record. Shut up, Matan. You've still got the eyewitness." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "That's Michael Parsons. The one that said 'kiss the floor.' He was convicted of four armed robberies in South Chicago over a two year period. He pled and was sentenced to Wabash Valley for four years. So, this is the killer? We believe so. Where is he now? Well, that's the bad news. He's dead. He died in prison in 2006." },
    { "sp": "Tara", "t": "I'm sorry. If I could help you, I would. But Clarence Wilcox did this. Just keep an open mind, please, Tara. This is the police sketch from the description you gave, right? And this is the mugshot of the man we think did it. Michael Parsons. Just please look at them carefully. I'm sorry. I really wish it was him. But it's not." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "This isn't Clarence Wilcox. Excuse me? We switched their faces. That's Michael Parsons. It is. This is Clarence Wilcox. And you just said that this man didn't do it. And this man did. My God. I am certain. The man I saw killing a police officer was not Clarence Wilcox." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Here's the thing, Mr. Brody. I don't like to be reversed. In fact, I hate it. Here's some advice for you and the good attorney. You keep that from happening. Tell Charles to blame his predecessor. It's not the job of the bench to offer you advice, Mr. Deputy Chief. But I am just telling you very clearly, I want this to go away." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "The regrettable corruption during Peter Florrick's term still infects this department. Every step in the Wilcox case was supervised and approved by Peter Florrick and we are doing everything in our power to correct his mistakes. That is why I have decided to withdraw all charges against Mr. Clarence Wilcox. There have been no discussions about restitution." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Yes. Thank you. No more questions. That is all. Thank you. They say he's coming out this door. Words are hard. I know. It's not like people say. I never expected him to get out." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "We can't risk it. Undue publicity argument is the weakest part of the brief. It needs more work. Which would be a problem if it was the only part of the brief. We've got five other solid arguments." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "We're never gonna shove all this in before the deadline. I got cable viewership from six years ago. Give me the numbers. It's not broken out by program. We're not going to make it. Damn? Don't say damn. This is Cary Agos at Stern, Lockhart & Gardner. We have a death row appeal coming to you now. Damn! WiFi's low!" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Mr. Gardener, please explain your undue publicity arguments. How is it different from the Scott Peterson appeal? It also involved a TV movie released prior to jury selection." },
    { "sp": "Patrice Wilcox", "t": "Do you know why Clarence wasn't in court today? Well, during automatic appeal, the defendant's not usually seated. Didn't someone tell you? So you're his new lawyers then? No. Legal Aid didn't have the staff to argue the appeal, so we're on temporarily. Because you never said anything about Clarence being innocent." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "To be honest, I did, too. So, what do we do now? Go back to the office. Well, about the case. The case? It's not ours. It's Legal Aid's." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Actual innocence? Yes. We lost the automatic appeal. Now you want to appeal on actual innocence? I read the trial record. Defense never put his wife on the stand. She was his only alibi. Because wives lie. I can't believe I'm having to tell you this. And she had priors. Juvenile priors. Then, those don't count. What am I missing here?" },
    { "sp": "Patrice Wilcox", "t": "Mrs. Florrick, thank you so much for doing this. Would you've pursued it if I hadn't given you pictures? That's what I said. Do you hand these out like party favors? Lawyers tend to forget he's a person. I love my husband. He's innocent, and I know he's innocent, and I would do anything for him. Anything." },
    { "sp": "Josh Baldwin", "t": "Now, I thought we had a good defense. We could have won. Except it was me against about a half-dozen Asas. This was the first case in that high-profile task force your husband set up. They only handled four or five cases a year, but they put all their resources into this. Why didn't you put Patrice on the stand? She had priors. The jury would think she was lying to protect her husband." },
    { "sp": "Tara", "t": "Was anything right in it? Well, I did see him Wilcox, that was true. He ran through the door, he fired his gun, he yelled at me to kiss the floor, but I peered up, and I saw him kill that police officer. You have no doubts? I've seen people die I a lot of people die. But I've only seen one person murdered. It's not something you forget." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Do you think we're on a fool's errand? I think we all have a job to do. I save the lives of some people who I know, as soon as they get well, are going to go out and kill. But I still try to save them. It's your job to try to get him off. It's the prosecutor's job to stop you. It's my job to tell the truth. I would say, 'Good luck,' but I wouldn't mean it." },
    { "sp": "Detective Shores", "t": "Hide everything, quick! Here comes the opposition. S'up, Muller. Hey, Shores. Busy day, I see. Hey, we're at a 65% clearance rate. We're doing our part to clean up after you. That was six years ago. Well, lady comes in, describes the suspect. Male, black, mid-20s, six feet tall, wearing a Bulls sweatshirt." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "Oh, my God. Yeah. The only one in a Bulls sweatshirt. They showed the eyewitness this six-pack before the lineup. I get this to Will. It's enough for an evidentiary appeal. Probably, but if you're going to go all the way with this, you'll have to talk to your husband. He knows where the bodies are buried." },
    { "sp": "Guard", "t": "Peter Florrick. Report to interview room five. Florrick, interview room five. Kalinda? Where's Alicia? Peter Florrick: Pat. Your wife doesn't want to visit you to get your take on this case. I don't deal in the same moral shades of black and white, so hi. What case? Clarence Wilcox. She's defending a cop killer. But he did it." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Yeah, that's the sweatshirt he was wearing. And you don't think it's prejudicial to put only one suspect wearing a Bulls sweatshirt in a photo lineup intended to find a suspect in a Bulls sweatshirt? And you found bloodstains on this sweatshirt? Here. The witness is pointing to the forearm of his right sleeve." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Let the record show that Detective Shores attempted to wipe his nose with the forearm of his right sleeve. Detective Shores, what were you preparing to do? Excuse me, Your Honor, he did nothing of the kind. Consistent with the stain on the accused's sweatshirt. Detective, I'm curious, if you got a nosebleed in a pickup game, what would you do?" },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Lenny, do you have one of these Bulls sweats? Judy, how about you? Anyone else? Your Honor, given this testimony, we request that Detective Shores' work product be excluded from the record." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "That's Michael Parsons. The one that said 'kiss the floor.' He was convicted of four armed robberies in South Chicago over a two year period. He pled and was sentenced to Wabash Valley for four years. So, this is the killer? We believe so. Where is he now? Well, that's the bad news. He's dead. He died in prison in 2006." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "So, they got an eyewitness and we got nothing. Great. That's great. Thank you for coming in, Doctor. Maybe you can. We have a picture to show you, we found another man we think is the killer. His name is Michael Parsons, and he was convicted of armed robberies too. He even used the same language from yours." }
  ],
  "S01E07": [
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "It's nothing. What do you mean, it's nothing? Look at them. I am looking at them. It's a financial meeting. That's how they look in a financial meeting. No, that's how they look when they're cutting jobs. Come on, law firms aren't recession-proof the way they used to be. Would you stop scaring yourself? They said billable hours are up this year." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "All right, let's cut four and hold on to two. Which four? Bayer? His wife is pregnant. He doesn't have seniority. See if he'll do part-time. And the junior associates? Let's put a pin in that. We don't have many pins left. Diane. Better late than never. What, Stern? The great man makes an appearance." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "It's not like law school, is it? It's better. I always felt unsure in law school. Really? Could have fooled me. I did fool you. So I have a favor to ask you. You never met M. Stern. Right. Anna's a real party girl. Her new husband hired some no-name lawyer, and M. Stern wants someone from our firm to cocounsel with him. Second chair him. Take Kalinda and get a lay of the land, okay?" },
    { "sp": "Anna Stern", "t": "Are you looking for me? Actually, we're— Are you? I'm Anna, Jonas Stern's daughter. I was in rehab out in Westchester, and there was this volunteer there, a Yeshiva student, and he had this amazing LP collection. And so you went Orthodox? They were good Lps. No, Isaac was talking about passion and music and the Torah, and I just I fell in love." },
    { "sp": "Anna Stern", "t": "Well, a lady tripped in front of our house and she's suing us. We have homeowner's insurance, but it only covers the $20,000 in compensatory damages, not the punitive. How much is the punitive? $1.2 million." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "Our firm feels that it could offer you some assistance. Odd, I didn't know I needed some assistance. And M. s. Loeb, could you give us a moment to talk? Ryan, if you could make this work, we'd prefer that. And Ms. Florrick, next time you see your husband, please tell him how much he is appreciated." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "So I do the depositions, the pretrial motions, prep for next week's trial, and here comes the 600-pound gorilla. Yep, that's me, 600 pounds. Did you read the brief? Nope. Talk to the plaintiff? Nope. Came on this case two hours ago. Our firm is doing it as a favor to M. s. Loeb. They saw the eruv wire was down, they knew it could trip somebody up, and they didn't try to repair it." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "That's why it's willful and wanton conduct and subject to high punitive damages. They admitted to that? In their depositions. And why would they admit to that? The wire fell on the Sabbath. They couldn't do anything. It's the perfect legal trap. They're liable because their religion wouldn't let them act. My defense is to suggest that you go tell your boss you're meeting regularly with me and I'm doing a great job and let me win this case." },
    { "sp": "Plaintiff", "t": "You were maintaining that injured so badly as to require a large judgment? I'm maintaining that I have pain every single day. I tried to ignore it. I tried to pick up my little girl, but I can't. It keeps me from pushing her on a swing. It keeps me from playing with her on the floor. And this grocery store was a half mile from your home. Why did you pass up five closer grocery stores to go to this one? My daughter needs gluten-free foods and this grocery store the kosher grocery store had more options." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Anything to undercut the injury testimony? I'm still checking. As far as I can tell, nobody witnessed her fall, but I'm checking with first responders. Any word from your cocounsel? No, our friend Mr. Alprin doesn't pick up his phone. This one's a tough one. The jury's gonna love her. And you're dealing with sympathy issues. Is that a euphemism for something? Anti-Semitism." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "So, here's your problem. You've got a client who admitted to liability, and a sympathetic plaintiff. You saw the depositions? Got them from the plaintiff's attorney. You wouldn't answer your phone. The only play I see is you attack the medical evidence, and try to reduce the punitive amount. What do you think of the First Amendment? I like it. Isaac and Anna's religion requires they not act on the fallen eruv wire, because it's the Sabbath. Therefore they can't be held liable for not acting." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You want to use the First Amendment to attack a slip and fall? They believed God would judge them if they acted, so they couldn't act. And what about OSHA v. Smith? OSHA v. Smith can kiss my ass. Liability is outweighed by the Loebs' right to exercise their religion. Up to what point? If they saw someone dying outside Well, yes, when that happens, I will call on you, Mrs. Florrick. But you're creating a loophole in liability law. No. The Constitution is." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "How long were you married? 15 years, and don't do that. What? Throw a personal question into the middle of an intellectual argument. It gets more honest answers. Judges in civil courts crave two things: novelty and getting out of civil court. The First Amendment is novel. We'd have to put a First Amendment scholar on the stand." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Stern called for an update on his daughter's case, he sent you something. He's in Bora-Bora. How's it going? Good. We're going for a First Amendment defense. On a slip and fall? Religious freedom trumps liability. Smart. Maybe I'll sneak into court, see how it's going. How's the cochair? He's interesting. Interesting is good." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Look, here's my take on it. We're too low on the totem pole to get fired because we don't cost enough. The problem is these older people. They're not hungry enough, you know? They cost too much. Cary, have you ever heard of a case here defending a toy company against lead poisoning? No. Why, should we be on it? No, no, it was two years ago. The kid died when we didn't settle." },
    { "sp": "Shoymer", "t": "Shoymer is a purely volunteer policing force for the Hasidim. Mostly we deal with hate crimes. And you were the first responders on the scene? Yeah, right here. Lady was on her back, screaming. On her back? But she fell forward. Yeah, and then she rolled on her back." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Stern costs just as much as 15 litigators, Diane. You want to make a move against Stern? I want to save 15 jobs. Stern brought us together. We're only a firm because of him. The wedding is over, the minister can go home. He costs us. He's not what he used to be. He doesn't bring in any clients. This is a power play, pure and simple." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "So, you are the owner and operator of Megalith Security. Is that right, sir? Yes, it is. And how do you know the plaintiff? Well, I don't know her. You're under oath, and you're saying you don't know her? Yes, I am. Fair enough. Sir What's this? It's a phone bill. It's your phone bill, correct? And this number here you see that? That number belongs to the plaintiff. So, was that a wrong number, sir? Probably. I don't know. And here on this page, did you phone that wrong number three more times? I don't know." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "All right, let's cut four and hold on to two. Which four? Bayer? His wife is pregnant. He doesn't have seniority. See if he'll do part-time. And the junior associates? Let's put a pin in that. We don't have many pins left. Diane. Better late than never. What, Stern? The great man makes an appearance. Jonas, how are you? We need to run some names by you for layoffs. Where are you? It sounds loud." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "It's not like law school, is it? It's better. I always felt unsure in law school. Really? Could have fooled me. I did fool you. So I have a favor to ask you. You never met M. Stern. Right. Anyway he has a daughter, Anna, from his first marriage. She's being sued. Her new husband hired some no-name lawyer, and M. Stern wants someone from our firm to cocounsel with him. Second chair him." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "We're going through a lot of changes around here. There's only three votes that really matter. Mine, Diane's, and M. Stern's. This is a good way to impress M. Stern. Thanks. You're not kidding." },
    { "sp": "Zach Florrick", "t": "Yeah, they were just on our doorstep. Someone rang the doorbell and ran. Why? I don't know, I think to scare my mom. Is that— What, crack? Yeah, but it's photoshopped. My dad's not even in the picture. Why do all the cool things happen to you? That's not the hooker from the sex tape? No, it's somebody else. If I could find her, I could figure out who took the pictures." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "What's the password for the parental controls? I don't have parental controls. My mom trusts me. You're kidding. This is so great. What are you doing? 39,000 matches. That's crazy. That's a start. You must be 18 years or older to access. Aren't you glad you have a friend like me to help you with a tough chore like this? No. Next site. This is going to take forever." },
    { "sp": "Anna Stern", "t": "Stern's daughter lives here? Maybe we have the wrong address. Or maybe there's a methadone clinic near here. Excuse me. Are you looking for me? Actually, we're— Are you? I'm Anna, Jonas Stern's daughter. I'm so sorry. Don't worry, I get that a lot. This is me now. I was in rehab out in Westchester, and there was this volunteer there, a Yeshiva student, and he had this amazing LP collection. And so you went Orthodox? They were good Lps." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Our firm feels that it could offer you some assistance. Odd, I didn't know I needed some assistance. M. And M. s. Loeb, could you give us a moment to talk? Yes, please. Ryan, if you could make this work, we'd prefer that. And Ms. Florrick, next time you see your husband, please tell him how much he is appreciated." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "So I do the depositions, the pretrial motions, prep for next week's trial, and here comes the 600-pound gorilla. Yep, that's me, 600 pounds. Did you read the brief? Nope. Talk to the plaintiff? Nope. Came on this case two hours ago. Our firm is doing it as a favor to M. s. Loeb. Why willful and? I was put on this case. It's not my choice and I really don't care if it's your choice. You call it a 600-pound gorilla or whatever you want, but now I'm your second chair." },
    { "sp": "Plaintiff", "t": "You were maintaining that injured so badly as to require a large judgment? I'm maintaining that I have pain every single day. I tried to ignore it. I tried to pick up my little girl, but I can't. It keeps me from pushing her on a swing. It keeps me from playing with her on the floor. And this grocery store was a half mile from your home. Why did you pass up five closer grocery stores to go to this one? My daughter needs gluten-free foods." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Good answer. Great answer. Anything to undercut the injury testimony? I'm still checking. As far as I can tell, nobody witnessed her fall, but I'm checking with first responders. Any word from your cocounsel? No, our friend Mr. Alprin doesn't pick up his phone. This one's a tough one. The jury's gonna love her. And you're dealing with sympathy issues. Is that a euphemism for something? Anti-Semitism. We'll deal with that in voir dire. Wonder what those questions will sound like 'Do you dislike Jews?' You're a bundle of fun today. You're kidding me! Layoffs." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "So, here's your problem. You've got a client who admitted to liability, and a sympathetic plaintiff. You saw the depositions? Got them from the plaintiff's attorney. You wouldn't answer your phone. The only play I see is you attack the medical evidence, and try to reduce the punitive amount. What do you think of the First Amendment? I like it. Isaac and Anna's religion requires they not act on the fallen eruv wire, because it's the Sabbath. Therefore they can't be held liable for not acting." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And what about OSHA v. Smith? OSHA v. Smith can kiss my ass. Liability is outweighed by the Loebs' right to exercise their religion. Up to what point? If they saw someone dying outside Well, yes, when that happens, I will call on you, Mrs. Florrick. But you're creating a loophole in liability law. No. The Constitution is." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "How long were you married? 15 years, and don't do that. What? Throw a personal question into the middle of an intellectual argument. It gets more honest answers. Judges in civil courts crave two things: novelty and getting out of civil court. The First Amendment is novel. We'd have to put a First Amendment scholar on the stand. I was about to make calls. Give me half." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Stern called for an update on his daughter's case, he sent you something. He's in Bora-Bora. How's it going? Good. We're going for a First Amendment defense. On a slip and fall? Religious freedom trumps liability. Smart. Maybe I'll sneak into court, see how it's going. How's the cochair? He's interesting. Interesting is good." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Look, here's my take on it. We're too low on the totem pole to get fired because we don't cost enough. The problem is these older people. They're not hungry enough, you know? They cost too much. Cary, have you ever heard of a case here defending a toy company against lead poisoning? No. Why, should we be on it? No, no, it was two years ago. The kid died when we didn't settle." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Two years ago, I had a case of lead poisoning in toys. Benny Brauer. Eight months old. Fell into a coma after sucking on a crib mobile. The insurance company offered to settle until your firm got involved. Isn't the point of representing clients to help them win? Meet me in open court, I'll beat you every time. That's not what your firm did. You buried us in paper, continuances, trial motions waiting for Benny Brauer to die." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "A week later, I heard your chief litigator had a name for it. 'Litigating the margins.' Delaying the reward to exhaust the opposition and therefore reduce the payout. We do pro bonos. We do death row cases. You can't tarnish an entire firm based on one case. Sure you can. Especially when one of the partners was the chief litigator. Will Gardner." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Stern costs just as much as 15 litigators, Diane. You want to make a move against Stern? I want to save 15 jobs. Stern brought us together. We're only a firm because of him. The wedding is over, the minister can go home. He costs us. He's not what he used to be. He doesn't bring in any clients. This is a power play, pure and simple. Get off it, Diane. Nothing here is pure, and nothing here is simple." },
    { "sp": "Shoymer", "t": "Shoymer is a purely volunteer policing force for the Hasidim. Mostly we deal with hate crimes. And you were the first responders on the scene? Yeah, right here. Lady was on her back, screaming. On her back? But she fell forward. Yeah, and then she rolled on her back." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You're kidding me! Layoffs. How can you work there? How can I work where? What are we talking about now? Stern, Lockhart & Gardner. Oh, right. The big bad corporate overlords. Much better to work in a storefront, fighting for Duis and prostitutes." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "You never met M. Stern. No, he's been away. Right. Barly and Barir Reef. Anyway he has a daughter, Anna, from his first marriage. She's being sued. I don't all the details, but I'm sure they're ugly. Last time, we got her out of a drug possession. The time before that, she drove a car through a department store window. You don't need to take notes on this. Anna's a real party girl. Her new husband hired some no-name lawyer, and M. Stern wants someone from our firm to cocounsel with him. With this other lawyer? Just make sure he doesn't screw up. Second chair him." },
    { "sp": "M. Alprin", "t": "They saw the eruv wire was down, they knew it could trip somebody up, and they didn't try to repair it. That's why it's willful and wanton conduct and subject to high punitive damages. They admitted to that? In their depositions. And why would they admit to that? The wire fell on the Sabbath. They couldn't do anything. It's the perfect legal trap. They're liable because their religion wouldn't let them act." }
  ],
  "S01E08": [
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Alicia we, we need you to do something that we didn't expect. Will you testify? Mrs. Florrick? Sorry to keep you waiting. We had to move them. I put them in the jury holding room." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Well, Russ, I'll always remember what my dad told me. And he said, 'if you're ever granted a second act in life, don't repeat the first one.' So as gratified as I am by all the talk, I think I think that's all it is: 'Talk'. I've got to go into that courtroom in the next few weeks and win this appeal." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "What drove you to seek prostitutes When you know it could put your life and your career in so much danger? I'm a flawed human being. And I've paid a heavy price. I'm not complaining. That's just the truth. But I've looked in the mirror, and what I've seen, I don't like. And I'm gonna change it." },
    { "sp": "Kya Poole", "t": "Hello, Mrs Florrick. I have a lot of respect for you. That's the look the public misses: Confident, real, optimistic. Know what he was talking about at that moment? You. Stern, Lockhart & Gardner, right? Say hello to will." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You work at Chicago Polytech, Isn't that correct, professor Whitton? More commonly known as cloning? Yes, cloning. Why does she keep apologizing so much? That's who she is." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Now, the prosecution contends that you buckled under the pressure of your research. You were supposed to deliver your results, and you missed several deadlines. Did you set the fire that destroyed your lab, professor Whitton? No. Never. I would never do anything to hurt my research." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "This is like watching a baby seal being clubbed. That's after how many sessions of witness prep? Three. You can't put her on the stand. The stammering, the nerves, she looks guilty. She's innocent. So's my four-year-old niece, but I wouldn't put her on the stand either. I'd keep her a mile away from the stand. You put her on, she's going away for 15 years." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Will, we're stretched a little thin here. The layoffs? I'll put Josephson on it. Keith Thomas, Arson expert. Mr. Thomas, can you give us your professional opinion of the Arson investigations? Thank you, sir." },
    { "sp": "Keith Thomas", "t": "It was a rush to judgment. The fire department labeled it an Arson, but the reality is, it could've been an accident. But Arson investigators said they found traces of lighter fluid at the crime scene. They're calling it lighter fluid, but what they really found was butane, which is not only commonplace in a lab, but could spontaneously ignite." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Aren't all the flammable liquids stored in steel cabinets on the other side of the lab? You believe that's correct, but you don't know. Just do your best. We're just trying to prep you for the tough questions. What about the wax residue found at the crime scene? Investigators say it came from a birthday candle used as part of a time-delay incendiary device. You ever seen that before?" },
    { "sp": "Ellen Whitton", "t": "You're doubting my story? No, we just feel we have better options. But if I don't testify, won't the jury think that I'm Suspicious? It's all about finding the best strategy for acquittal, ellen. They've taken my passport. Yes, well, that's, Standard. It feels like I'm just being put in a smaller and smaller box." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Cary, you continue with witness prep. Alicia, you help him. Try to keep it competitive. Plan B: Religious fanatics. Saw your husband's appeal starts today. Should be interesting. I'm kind of nostalgic for when things weren't so interesting." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "You know, I've never lived on a farm, but I think that's what it looks like when the weather vane changes direction. A good change? If the assistant state's attorney is coming to me, A great change. There's only half a dozen pieces of hate mail. Cops didn't consider them any serious threat. But I need a suspect." },
    { "sp": "Judge Harvey Winter", "t": "All rise for the honorable judge harvey winter. Mr. Florrick, it's been a long time. Always a pleasure, your honor. Mr. Golden, we would first like to schedule an evidentiary hearing to present new exculpatory evidence. Two weeks from today: 3:00. I would also like you to consider releasing my client on electronic monitoring while the appeal is ongoing." },
    { "sp": "Mr. Landry", "t": "Your honor, bail should not even be on the table at this Bail hearing is set for day after tomorrow 5:00. The state vehemently objects. Your vehemence is noted. There will be a bail hearing here, day after tomorrow, at 5:00, Mr. Landry. You're welcome to join us." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "How about that? I could be out in two days. See you. Soon." },
    { "sp": "Zach Florrick", "t": "So, is he getting out? How did you? It was online. There's 65 articles. One even in Singapore. So is he going to live here? He'll be on electronic monitoring. He'll be stuck here." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "So if any of them seem remotely legit, just Tell them two minutes. This one, the Christian Coalition for Bioethics. They're legit. They're radical religious bio-cons opposed to genetic engineering, cloning, stem cell research. They've also been known to put coded messages in their letters. Someone behind this has a real zodiac complex." },
    { "sp": "Mary Bennett", "t": "It'll just feel scripted in court. No, I get it. You want me natural. What is your connection to this case? Well, I work the front desk at the biochem lab. I work with professor Whitton. I'm in the physics department. She's in chemistry. I saw her walk out of the lab at 7:00 PM. And she didn't return? Yes." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "So, next time, warn her when you're flipping from advisor to prosecutor. Do we have a problem? We have a problem if you're trying to show me up at the expense of witness prep. Okay. You're right. I'm sorry. Ellen is a lovely person, And a great colleague." },
    { "sp": "Walt Gifford", "t": "What? I thought she needed me. Yes, I am. No storytelling. No embellishments. 'Yes' means yes. 'No' means no. Where were you the night of the arson, walt? I was at the auditorium for the piano recital. It started at 8:30 in auditorium number four. Left around 9:00 to make a phone call. To whom? My mother. And that's when I saw ellen across the street, at the track, running." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "So, a letter written by the christian coalition for bioethics should have all the hallmarks of a protestant terrorist group: the logo, the cryptic apropos bible quote. So why did they pull a quote from the douay-rheims edition, a catholic bible? I think it's a red herring; I think someone created it to throw the blame on somebody else. I think you might have someone in mind." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Well, if I were a cynic, I'd say our client. She is the one who found a letter on her windshield. You are a cynic. Yep. Did you see anyone while you were in front of the recital hall? Yes. I saw Ellen Whitton, running the track across the street. And then the fire trucks raced by to the lab. On University avenue? Yes, that's correct." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "But University avenue Isn't the most direct route From the fire station to the lab. They'd simply thank us and bury it. You know one option, if you want to really be gutsy? Nail him on the stand. Trap him in his own lies. And if we do it wrong, we just proved our alibi false. Hey, no pain, no gain." },
    { "sp": "Walt Gifford", "t": "I arrived just as the performance began at 8:30. Around 9:00, I stepped outside to phone my mother right in front of the recital hall. The recital hall scrambles the signals, calls don't interfere with performance, So how could you have gotten through? There's no record of that call in your cell phone records. No record of any call at all. You weren't at the recital, Walt." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You were at the lab, waiting to set the fire, destroy Ellen's research, and frame her for it. Why? If I wanted to frame her, why would I be here, vouching for her? Is it because you set the fire to keep her here instead of off at the Cambridge fellowship? But you never meant to have it pinned on her, did you? And when it was, you tried to save her. With an alibi. With fake letters from a hate group." },
    { "sp": "Judge Harvey Winter", "t": "All rise for the Honorable Judge Harvey Winter. Before I rule on bail, I'm told the prosecution intends to make a proffer to the court. The state has recently received evidence that the defendant has attempted to interfere with the judicial process. Bail is hereby denied. This court is adjourned." },
    { "sp": "Mr. Landry", "t": "I have a signed affidavit from asa Alex Phelan stating that Peter Florrick has asked him to influence Your Honor into granting bail. Now we know your honor is truly impartial, and that any influence would have landed on deaf ears, But we also believe that mr. Florrick has put you in an awkward position, confusing the public as to the impartiality of any bail offer you might extend." },
    { "sp": "Daniel Golden", "t": "Mr. Florrick, let me quickly say, You have a fifth amendment right against self-incrimination. Mr. Gifford, if you don't mind me saying, There is some Ambivalence inherent in your position, Alicia. And ambivalence could hurt Peter's chances. The truth is the truth, but it can often sound Truer." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I'm not going to lie on the stand, Daniel. Is the judge in Peter's pocket? No. I'll see you in court. Mrs. Florrick, you mentioned the apartment is a three bedroom, and you have two children. Would they share a room? No. So, you and your husband would share a room? Yes. I'm simply establishing that there truly is a place for mr. Florrick, given everything that's— This is a private family matter, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Mr. Landry", "t": "You're saying under oath that you intend to share a bed with— Objection, your honor. Relevance. I'm suggesting you stop asking invasive and irrelevant questions. Isn't it true you met with a divorce attorney on February 23? Yes. I had a brief consultation with a divorce attorney a few days after the allegations against my husband surfaced. To weigh my options. You haven't ruled it out? I haven't ruled out running for president." },
    { "sp": "Judge Harvey Winter", "t": "Any further questions? Why don't we leave it there? I'll take the arguments and testimony into consideration and rule tomorrow. Mrs. Florrick, I have one more question. Did you meet with a divorce attorney on February 23? The relevant question here Is whether Peter has a suitable place to go home to. And I am stating, under oath, that he does." },
    { "sp": "Russ", "t": "And I'd do it again. But I do wish that every prosecutor Had my experience of walking in a convict's shoes. Well, you must've seen the articles or heard the rumblings. With crime rates up and disappointment in your successor rising, The voters seem to want you back." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Hey, you'll drop by later, right? Good. I'm sorry. What was the question again?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "That puts more pressure on the other witnesses. Get some tougher witness prep, will ya? Those were wiffle balls you were throwing at her. The layoffs? I need it to be tougher. More blood sport. You get two lawyers who really butt heads, make them prosecution-defense. You need this to be more darwinian, more real world. Doesn't get more darwinian than that. Witness prep 108." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "He was the best. We had three other Arson experts, all half as good. Well You know how I'm always looking for a silver lining. The bad news is, we can't sell this as an accident. Wax paper and spontaneous combustion? Jurors will laugh you out of court. So if we can't sell it as an accident, Sell it as Arson, just not by ellen." },
    { "sp": "Ellen Whitton", "t": "When does your family arrive? They're not coming. The last continuance, they lost money on the plane tickets, and I just told them not to come. I don't know anyone here. I'll be right back. You can go, too, if you want. It's all right. It's not. I'm facing 15 years in prison." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Left-wing bioconservatives, religious bioconservatives, Animal rights activists Ellen's lab received hate mail from all of them. Okay, good. Diane: Cary, you continue with witness prep. Alicia, you help him. Try to keep it competitive. Tamara, keep me in the loop." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "Saw your husband's appeal starts today. Should be interesting. I'm kind of nostalgic for when things weren't so interesting. You need a drink. Probably. Mike: Hey, Mike. Judy, you look great. Bob: Hey, Bob, Alex. So, you've gained a few pounds. I heard you, made some new friends in tamms. Yeah, a nice burglar. I gave him your address. You believe that? Peter: I hired Landry. Taught him everything he knows. Let's just hope you didn't teach him too well." },
    { "sp": "Mr. Golden", "t": "Peter Florrick was tried, convicted, Sentenced. There's no longer any presumption of innocence. This is not about innocence. This is about bond. Peter Florrick has no previous record, he is not a flight risk. He is a family man who is eager to go home to his wife and children, who are eager to have him." },
    { "sp": "Zach Florrick", "t": "You're still in trouble. I know. I have to get you home, And then I have to check an alibi. What is that? This? Nothing. Homework." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "I ran an ink x-ray analysis on the fake ccb letters. The toner is office store brand 4546-h. Very unique. And one of the few places that uses it Is the printer at Ellen's office. Okay, well, that's disappointing. Yep, disappointing she's sending threats to herself." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "How could Walt phone his mom from the auditorium door if it's a dead zone? Check this out. Walt said the fire trucks came here, on University av, but the fire station is there and the lab is there. Garden av is a direct route. Why would the firefighters go the long way around? They wouldn't. The physics department?! The whole science department orders in bulk." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Alex Phelan was at Zach's baptism. Could he have misinterpreted? It's a betrayal. I thought I'd be leaving with you. Mr. Florrick. You'll tell the kids? I will. I'll see you. This was a set-up. Peter didn't make bail. So, who is it? Zach: I don't know. But What's he doing? He's taking pictures of our front door. Why? I don't know." }
  ]
,
  "S01E09": [
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Bail is hereby denied." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "This court is adjourned." },
    { "sp": "Talk Show Host", "t": "Peter didn't make bail." },
    { "sp": "Talk Show Host", "t": "My next guest has spent quality time with some of the biggest political names in Chicago politics, and she's working on a shocking tell-all book." },
    { "sp": "Talk Show Host", "t": "Please welcome Amber Madison." },
    { "sp": "Amber Madison", "t": "Hi, how are you? So nice to meet you." },
    { "sp": "Amber Madison", "t": "State's attorney." },
    { "sp": "Talk Show Host", "t": "Okay. Right, whatever, this D. A. so now, did he suck on your toes all the time or was that just, like, a one-time shot?" },
    { "sp": "Amber Madison", "t": "It was a Two-time thing." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Look, Amber Madison is yesterday's news." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Thank you. She'd say anything to sell a book." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "It doesn't warrant a comment." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "He's trying his wife." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "I usually don't ask this, but how much is true?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "From what I can tell, not much." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Are you okay?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "No." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "That's good, the genuine is important right now. Unless you can get me in touch with my wife, I think you should shut up." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Courtney, you got a minute?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Courtney. Just wait. So now we're getting to a comfortable place. Thank you. Now, what kind of code are we talking about here?" },
    { "sp": "Amber Madison", "t": "Well, I would call Peter's house and hang up after one ring, and then he would know that the next call was going to be from me." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Anyway, so about that threesome It was nothing. It he said that it was the reason that he came to me in the first place. His wife wouldn't agree to a threesome. I think he thought that she was a little Frigid." },
    { "sp": "Amber Madison", "t": "You can say it. I mean, everyone I've seen her, I think she looks frigid." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "This is beyond damage control. Don't let's get into that now, please." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "What? We can't just let this sit. You're like Fletcher Christian Just itching to throw bligh overboard." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Yes, and Mr. Christian was the hero." },
    { "sp": "News Anchor", "t": "Attorneys at Stern, Lockhart & Gardner have had no comment on this latest embarrassment. It appears that Jonas Stern's single car accident caused no injuries. Stern, Chicago's renowned civil rights attorney, First gained notoriety crusading against police brutality - in the 1970s." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "So, where is that jackass Kreutzer? It's over, you know that." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "His clients are going to mutiny. Stern's clients understand his proclivities." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "I'm thinking of defending myself." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Jonas, you are not going to defend yourself. It's trumped up, you know that. All that time I spent on the police review board, The Chicago cops can't wait to knock me down a peg. This is your second offense. Worst-case scenario, I lose my license for a year." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "Four scotch and sodas. I was blitzed. After the accident. I crashed, I was shook up. I walked into the Westwood tavern and downed down four scotch and sodas. God. The police are going to have a hard time discerning the drinking I did before and the drinking I did after." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "Yes. I want her. I want her as a front. I'll defend myself through her." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "I want her to represent me." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "The best arguments are spontaneous." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "Justice Powell. The oral arguments for Ricardo v. Ohio." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I did my law review on Ricardo. I was brilliant on Ricardo, wasn't I?" },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "You were. It's harder doing great these days. Fewer opportunities. I chased you out of the gossip columns, didn't I?" },
    { "sp": "Clerk", "t": "Your Honor, Matan Brody of the State's attorney's office." },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "The people have added a charge to the complaint, Your Honor. Aggravated battery to a police officer." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "That's ridiculous. You tacked that on for payback." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Are you representing yourself here?" },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "Your honor, this is payback." },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "Aggravated battery is a felony, Your honor, carrying a prison term of two to five years. As such, both this charge and the dui now have to be heard in felony court." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Mr. Stern, I heard you speak at Georgetown once, and you said, \"even the best legal mind requires the socratic method to keep itself sharp\" So Here I am." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "So, you had a few drinks at the party before the accident." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "I had one drink, and they don't know that." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Because, after the accident, you had a few more?" },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "That's perfectly legal. You blew a . 15 on the breathalyzer at the police station. It's twice the legal limit." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And the battery against the officer? No mention in the police report. Now, these police cars They've got their video cameras right on the dash. Those recordings are kept for up to six days. Subpoena those recordings before they can erase them." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Yes, ms. Florrick?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Given Illinois State Supreme Court." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "Yes, Your Honor, it was." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "That case dealt with video from an outside source. This is video from the officer's dashboard. We're arguing that the underlying principle is the same, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "That might work with the Illinois Supreme Court, but not here. Very good. We'll reconvene after lunch." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "We move to disqualify the video on the grounds that it was obtained as the result of an illegal arrest." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Your Honor, I am Mr. Stern's legal representative." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Officer Sutton stated on his dashboard video, \"Don't make me put the handcuffs back on you\" Implying that he'd been handcuffed previously. Did you, officer Sutton?" },
    { "sp": "Officer Sutton", "t": "I'd handcuffed him when I found him In the bar because he was belligerent, as you see in the video. But when I brought him outside, I took the cuffs off - so I could perform a sobriety test." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Then it was an arrest, your honor. Mr. Stern was not free to go. And officer Sutton had no probable cause The mere fact that it was an accident Does not mean a crime was committed. So, everything that follows is tainted The sobriety tests, the video." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Motion granted. The video is hereby excluded. We're adjourned until tomorrow." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "Well, well, The best arguments really do sound spontaneous. Mr. Stern, You cannot backseat drive your own defense. You chose me to represent you. If that is what you want, I'm your lawyer. You're my client. Let's go." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Donepezil. It's the name of the medicine you take. That's why you crashed. You didn't drink at the party, and the roads weren't wet." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "What are you talking about?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I saw them, Mr. Stern. They're not energy pills. Peter's father had dementia, not alzheimer's. His was vascular. I know what it looks like. The forgetfulness, the anger, mood swings." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You know nothing. Jonas Stern does not have dementia." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "I'll be damned if I'm gonna have people think that jonas stern is losing his mind! There are three people who know what you know. One is my doctor, the second one is me, And the third is now covered by attorney-client privilege. And as your client, I am ordering you not to breathe a word of this. Not in my defense, not to anyone. Do you understand? Do you understand?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I understand." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Officer, what sobriety tests did you perform on Mr. Stern?" },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Mr. Brody, I'm dismissing the dui. Unless you want me to dismiss the battery and levy sanctions, You'd better come up with something fast." },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "Misdemeanor assault. Six months probation." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "Ask her. Misdemeanor assault? Six months probation? Time served? You must be pretty pleased with yourself. You shouldn't be driving, Mr. Stern. And unless you disclose your condition, you shouldn't be practicing law, either." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "I'm not fighting you, Diane. I'm leaving. Last thing I need is to waste time and energy here fighting with people I love. And I do. That thing I said earlier I'm sorry. It wasn't true. I know. I was the best lawyer you had at that firm." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "Who said anything about retiring? I'm leaving to start a new firm." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "You know, last year, you billed over $50 million between Gentech financials and Parthenon systems alone. Now, they're my clients, - and I'm taking them with me." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Jonas, you take our clients, you forfeit your golden parachute." },
    { "sp": "Stern", "t": "My clients. And that's not all. I'm gonna take a third of your business. And then I'm coming back for the rest. But, hey, It'll be fun." },
    { "sp": "Daniel Golden", "t": "Your husband was set up." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "What?!" },
    { "sp": "Daniel Golden", "t": "Forget the sex. Sex was just the barker's tease. There are a lot of people who made a bad bet, And now they're covering their ass." },
    { "sp": "Daniel Golden", "t": "Mr. Stern That's it, Alicia. I'm not your deep throat on this. Your husband was set up, and they're not going to let him out of prison ever." }
  ],
  "S01E10": [
    { "sp": "Prosecutor", "t": "Simple assault." },
    { "sp": "Prosecutor", "t": "Six months' detention, three years' probation." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Two years' probation, no jail time." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You do know it's called bargaining, right? Why am I the only one making concessions? I gave you an extra year on probation." },
    { "sp": "Prosecutor", "t": "You're not giving me anything on detention." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Because my client doesn't deserve it He's a 98-pound wallflower." },
    { "sp": "Prosecutor", "t": "Who clocked a classmate with an algebra textbook." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Because he was being bullied." },
    { "sp": "Prosecutor", "t": "19 stitches, a cracked eye socket." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "One of our big clients; it's his housekeeper's son." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Look, my client is a straight-a student. Never had a bad day before, never have a bad day again, unless you incarcerate him." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "One year probation, and he takes responsibility in open court. No \"bully did this, I did that\"." },
    { "sp": "Prosecutor", "t": "Thank you. Go and sin no more." },
    { "sp": "Committee Rep", "t": "Diane We want you to be a judge." },
    { "sp": "Committee Rep", "t": "We're here as representatives of the democratic ward and committee chairs, and we would like to slate you for the upcoming election." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "I'm startled." },
    { "sp": "Chief Justice Adler", "t": "And my firm? You'll have to divest, of course. We don't do the smoke-filled rooms anymore, but we do manage the primary ballot. So if you agree, you'll be the only woman slated, and thus, a shoo-in." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Six months' probation, a hundred hours' community service. You're lucky it's Christmastime, Mr. Thompson. Defecate again in your neighbor's closet, I won't be so jolly." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "And who's our next contestant, Danny?" },
    { "sp": "Danny", "t": "Terrence Ramsay, your honor." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Where's your counselor, young man?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Here, your honor." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Good! Shall we all gather around the campfire here, counselor? I understand you've reached a plea agreement, Mr. Richardson?" },
    { "sp": "Mr. Richardson", "t": "Yes, your honor. Mr. Ramsay has agreed to plead guilty to simple assault." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Is that correct? You're ready to plead guilty?" },
    { "sp": "Terrence", "t": "Yes, sir." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "To the charge of simple assault, how do you plead, Mr. Ramsay?" },
    { "sp": "Terrence", "t": "Guilty, your honor." },
    { "sp": "Terrence", "t": "At lunch, Reggie said he was gonna beat me up after school. He called me a little bitch. And he said he was gonna break my glasses. He scared me, and I threw my book at him." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "And the state's recommendation?" },
    { "sp": "Mr. Richardson", "t": "One year probation and 200 hours of community service." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "You caused some serious injuries, Mr. Ramsay. Broken eye socket, stitches" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "The other child is recovering quite well, your honor." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Break a nose, and your punishment is picking up trash?" },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "The agreement that you have, Mr. Ramsay, is between you and the government, and as such, I am not party to that agreement and can impose a harsher sentence. Do you understand that?" },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "I am sentencing you to nine months' detention. You will receive the guidance you need to become a citizen, and come to understand that actions - have consequences." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Your honor This will be the best thing, I promise. I'm sorry, but we had an agreement: No jail time." },
    { "sp": "Howard Brightman", "t": "Howie." },
    { "sp": "Howard Brightman", "t": "Baxter is a bipolar sentencer, but nobody wants to confront him because we all gotta go back there." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "These are all from legal aid. Baxter's cases. On this side, everybody got probation. On that, jail time." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "He's a racist." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "He's best buds with Baxter." },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "We don't know, but something in June changed for Baxter." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "So I'm biased against African-Americans?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Your honor, we looked at five years" },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Did you consider the education of the defendant in your little statistical jeremiad? Did you consider family structure, community services, quality of representation? No. You went right to race?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "My motion didn't single you out, sir." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Send Mr. Ramsay home or else." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "They arrested a guy who fit the description mrs. Baxter gave them, but she wouldn't come in to I. D. She wasn't up to it. Baxter asked them to let it go." },
    { "sp": "Chief Justice Adler", "t": "You have a junior associate accusing him of racism." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "No, biased sentencing." },
    { "sp": "Chief Justice Adler", "t": "Judges don't go up against each other, Diane. You can't question - a sitting judge." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Henry baxter. Everyone out there is pussyfooting around you because you're his friend. I said no." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I know he's not a racist. But something did happen, Will. He started sentencing more harshly last June. You're his best friend. So help me. What happened?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "He borrowed some money, that's all." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "How much?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "About $120,000. He said he was in a tight spot. Gambling. But he was gonna make it right." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "That's why he dropped the charges. It wasn't rape, he knew the attacker." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "So is that when you arranged the kickbacks? The money you gave henry for sending kids to you." },
    { "sp": "Palgrave Admin", "t": "Officer michael, could you show these two out?" },
    { "sp": "Kalinda Sharma", "t": "You better start looking for a good defense attorney, pal!" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "You look good in action." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "How much does palgrave pay you per kid? How much?" },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Palgrave is a good place for rehabilitation. These kids are better off there." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Did you even just say the words to yourself to see how they sounded? \"I'm sending kids to prison to make money. \" It's not even about doing good. Nobody here is helping orphans, but this, how can you even live a day with this?" },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "Don't you dare judge me. You. The litigator who whored himself out to the lowest scum. Don't you dare judge me." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "You're going to jail." },
    { "sp": "Judge Baxter", "t": "What?!" }
  ],
  "S01E11": [
    { "sp": "David Lee", "t": "Family law." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Oh, right. I'm so sorry. You're the divorce lawyer." },
    { "sp": "David Lee", "t": "Yes, I'm the divorce lawyer." },
    { "sp": "David Lee", "t": "I need you at a meet and greet in 45 minutes to hand-hold a client." },
    { "sp": "David Lee", "t": "See you in 45 minutes." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I'm tired of being the one who has to hand-hold the clients." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I've worked two months on this Duke case, and now that they're settling, I'm off on some divorce case, and Cary gets all the Glory." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "We would like to move on." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You mean from Cheryl Willens' suicide or the loss of ten sponsors?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "That wasn't selling; that was rubbing it in. Over the phone, will and I talked about $2 million." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Actually, we talked about $3 million and a retraction." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "The network might be able to go to 2.5, but any retraction would have to be worked out separately with Duke." },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "I'm curious... what is the amount? How much you guys paying?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "$2.5 million." },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "Whoa. Wow. Is Mr. Willens gonna be paying that out of his own pocket, or is your law firm gonna be helping him out?" },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "The network would prefer to handle this... quietly. Love quietly." },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "If the network pays one red commie cent, I'm gonna quit the network, and I'm gonna take my nine million viewers with me." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "All rise for the honorable judge Abernathy." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Uh, no, no. Please, everybody, sit." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "My goodness. We're crowded today." },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "I think that, uh, has something to do with me, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Yes, Mr. Roscoe, I do believe you're right." },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "Well, you're welcome." },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "Thank you, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Your Honor, is Duke representing himself here?" },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "No, no, it's all right, Mr. Gardner. A smidgen of informality is, uh, allowed us all." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "And yes, Mr. Roscoe, I clearly admit that we probably would agree on very little, but this court is a blind court, and I will endeavor to be fair." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Your honor, the defense moves for a summary dismissal based on the first amendment." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "The plaintiff would deny Mr. Roscoe the right to speak his mind... no, your honor. We want to deny Mr. Roscoe the right to slander and lie." },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "The truth is an absolute defense. I've been nothing but truthful about that child killer." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Okay, counselors, thank you. Uh, a second." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "Mr. Gardner, you argue that the first amendment guarantees the right to speak but not the right to lie. I agree with that statement." },
    { "sp": "Judge Abernathy", "t": "But our case will be decided on very narrow grounds, Mr. Gardner, Ms. Lockhart." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Okay, Cary, Kalinda, this now turns into a fact-finding case." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Who was leaking Duke all his information in the police investigation?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Oh, and, Alicia, you keep on Tim. Hand-hold him, make sure he stays on the reservation." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Actually, Alicia needs to split her time with the childs' divorce." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Okay, Cary, you stay with him. Hand-hold him." },
    { "sp": "David Lee", "t": "Do both parties understand?" },
    { "sp": "David Lee", "t": "We seem to be only one party." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You signed a prenuptial agreement, Mrs. Childs. The only matter to discuss is when are you moving out." },
    { "sp": "Carla Browning", "t": "The matter to discuss is the amount of Carla's spousal support and whether she's going to allow Mr. Childs to visit his children at all." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Why would he do that, Mrs. Childs? Because of her." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Tell your client that if he doesn't make it to our next arbitration, I will tell Mrs. Florrick what I found on his laptop." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "You're poisoning her against me." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You want to talk to me, you phone my assistant and you make an appointment, but don't you ever come into my office without my permission." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Carla was happy until you talked to her." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Mr. Roscoe, on your show of August 15, did you say, \"Jamie Willens was never seen on the convenience store video because her mother killed her earlier that night.\"" },
    { "sp": "Duke Roscoe", "t": "You have the transcript right there in your hand, Mr. Gardner. I do, don't I? There you are. Yes, to the best of my memory, Mr. Gardner, I said the words you just there quoted." }
  ],
  "S01E12": [
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Alicia. Hi, sorry. It's Will." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I know you just got home," },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "but I just got a call from memorial North." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "The hospital?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Yeah. We signed them a month ago." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "I need someone to get over there, make sure if this does turn into a malpractice suit, we got our ducks in a row." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Look, I know we've been asking a lot of you lately." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "So, if you want me to put this on Cary, I can." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "No. I've got it." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Thank God." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Dr. Wesley." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "May I speak to you for a moment?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Please." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "Do I know you?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I'm Alicia." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I'm a lawyer with Stern, Lockhart & Gardner." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You can't be talking with the mother right now." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "Why not?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Your patient overdosed from pills you prescribed." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And there is some question about the dosage." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Even a simple expression of sympathy could be construed as an admission of guilt." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "Then it's an admission." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "That's my patient. I'm their family doctor." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And I'm your lawyer." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "If I was sick right now, I would do exactly as you told me to do." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "But you're in trouble." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And I need you to do exactly as I tell you." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "I... I'm in trouble? The police think...?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Please." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Where's your office?" },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "The south tower." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I want you to go there." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Don't speak to anyone." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I know this is going to be difficult, but promise me, please..." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "I'd never put Ben in harm's way." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "If you're my attorney, I want you to know that." },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "This is going to be huge, isn't it?" },
    { "sp": "Dr. Wesley", "t": "The press, people..." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Block it out." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I'm so sorry, Mrs. Bowers." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I'm... I'm just trying to piece together what happened." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Bowers", "t": "No, you're not." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Bowers", "t": "You're trying to protect the doctor who killed my son." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Bowers", "t": "He was a good boy." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Bowers", "t": "You should be trying to fight for him." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I just want to know the truth." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Bowers", "t": "No, you don't." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Bowers", "t": "You're a lawyer." }
  ],
  "S01E13": [
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Okay, now, I know you're all are excited about this trial, but this is a probate matter, and, as such, you're just visiting here. I live here. My rules go." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Mr. Gardner, are you ready?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Yes, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Mr. Cantwell?" },
    { "sp": "Nancy Crozier", "t": "Nancy Crozier... Your Honor. Mr. Cantwell has taken sick, and the plaintiff has asked me to step in." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Miss Armitage?" },
    { "sp": "Mariel Armitage", "t": "Yes." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "You can request a continuance if you would like to look for other representation. As you can see, the defense has spared no expense." },
    { "sp": "Mariel Armitage", "t": "Yes, uh, I know, Your Honor, but Nancy and I go way back, and I think I'd rather go with someone I trust." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Ma'am, you're not old enough to go way back, but if, uh, Miss... What is it? Crozier. Crozier is your choice... Come on up here, Miss Crozier." },
    { "sp": "Nancy Crozier", "t": "Okay." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "That's where you sit." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Miss Crozier?" },
    { "sp": "Nancy Crozier", "t": "I'm sorry. Could you...?" },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Do you have an argument?" },
    { "sp": "Nancy Crozier", "t": "I don't know." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Well, if you don't know, then I must decide against you." },
    { "sp": "Nancy Crozier", "t": "Well, the one thing that I would say, Your Honor, is that... that I do think that it would be fairer to have a cross section of people, not just one. Please don't take that the wrong way." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Miss Crozier?" },
    { "sp": "Nancy Crozier", "t": "That might be true, but I'm not going to try and, uh... Rile up those passions. I just... I want them to hear the truth." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Well, you've won your first argument, Miss Crozier. We'll start choosing a jury tomorrow morning." },
    { "sp": "Nancy Crozier", "t": "Thank you." },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "Mrs. Florrick. A pleasure to meet you." },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "Don't worry. I killed her with my other hand. Joking. Sorry. I've developed a perverse sense of the comic over the last year." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Okay, well, let's try not to joke like that in court, shall we?" },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "You think it'll come to that... court? Cantwell is a top litigator... he wants a trial. Our goal is to avoid a jury." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "You're in a similar situation to O.J., if you don't mind me continuing the metaphor." },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "Like O.J., you were found not guilty, but people think you did it, and they want to see you hurt. Your stepdaughter is counting on that that's why she's suing you in civil court." },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "Charlotte... we never saw eye to eye, I'm afraid. She resented Carolyn leaving her father for me." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Unfortunately, the Illinois slayer statute says you can't inherit someone's estate if you cause their death. If Charlotte wins, she gets the money, the company." },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "What do you think, Mrs. Florrick?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "What do I think about what?" },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "Your husband prosecuted me. You must have an opinion as to my guilt or innocence." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "My opinion is irrelevant." },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "Not to me. Not at this moment. You think I killed my wife?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Of course." },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "Look, you pay us to represent your interests..." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Even though her body was never found?" },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "I'm sure you found ways to dispose of it. Chopped her up and buried her in a landfill somewhere? Or in Lake Michigan." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And my alibi? That you were driving from St. Louis to Chicago at the time?" },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "So you have no problem representing a murderer?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Oh, I have a great problem with it." },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "But you'll do it anyway?" },
    { "sp": "Colin Sweeney", "t": "Unless you'd like to exclude me." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Oh, not for the world." },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "What do you want?" },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "You do understand I'll be a hostile witness?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "I respected you. Childs has turned you into an errand boy." },
    { "sp": "Matan Brody", "t": "Mr. Florrick, you hired me. I'm grateful for that. You ran a tough department. I admire that. But the day you started slipping your wife information to use in court, that's the day I lost respect for you..." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Your witness." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Thank you, Your Honor. Uh, actually, just to be clear, you are the defendant's sister-in-law, Mariel?" },
    { "sp": "Mariel Armitage", "t": "The defendant who killed my sister?" },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "We all need to perform our roles here, Mr. Gardner." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Yes, you need to object, and, yes, I need to sustain." },
    { "sp": "Mariel Armitage", "t": "I'm sorry, Your Honor. We won't let that happen again." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "You and the defendant, Mr. Sweeney, had sexual relations?" },
    { "sp": "Mariel Armitage", "t": "Yeah." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "He had sex with his wife's - sister?" },
    { "sp": "Mariel Armitage", "t": "Yeah, that's correct." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Uh, Miss Armitage, what could possibly have led you to sleep with your own sister's husband?" },
    { "sp": "Mariel Armitage", "t": "I struggled with addiction my whole life, and my sister and I were estranged over it. So I suppose I... I don't know... I did it just to get back at her. And Colin was too happy to help. He used to make me do things. You know, things his wife wouldn't do. Like the time he had me tie up his... I think that that is enough. I'm just going to sit down. Thank you." }
  ],
  "S01E14": [
    { "sp": "Tony", "t": "Jason Rucker." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "Yes?" },
    { "sp": "Tony", "t": "Mr. Rucker, you are under arrest for the murder of Lisa Pruitt." },
    { "sp": "Tony", "t": "You have the right to remain silent." },
    { "sp": "Tony", "t": "Anything you say can and will be used against you in a court of law." },
    { "sp": "Tony", "t": "You have the right to an attorney." },
    { "sp": "Sonya", "t": "Jason! Jason!" },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "Sonya!" },
    { "sp": "Tony", "t": "Ma'am, no, you need to step back." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Mr. Rucker, do you have a second?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "It's not a knife. Those aren't deep enough for knife wounds. Looks like a blunt instrument." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "But I just saw her." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "You just saw her... the babysitter..." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "When you picked her up?" },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "Yes. I picked her up at her dorm, and I drove her to..." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "I'm in trouble? I'm the last one that saw her." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "They'll suspect me?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Yes." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "Oh, my God. Then why...?" },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "I need to go to the police, don't I?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "I just spoke to them. I agreed to surrender you in the morning, but these first few hours of an investigation are always the most important." },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "We're going to have you take a polygraph, Mr. Rucker." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "It's not about truth or innocence." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "I'm sorry, a lie detector test?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Yes, I need information so I know how much to let you talk versus how much to keep you silent." },
    { "sp": "Jason Rucker", "t": "How long?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "20 minutes, ten if they send someone from central. You have 15 minutes. I'm going to start hooking you up." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "So, you know the offer you made?" },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "What offer would that be?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Freedom today if I don't fight the conviction, stay disbarred, can't run again." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Yes, that offer. Smart move would be to take it." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "So I have to imagine you're going to tell me to go to hell." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "You want the longer version?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Can't wait." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "I wouldn't get too comfortable in that office of yours. I'm gonna testify." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Would it help if I told you that that's a mistake?" },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Look, if I end up in a 12 by 12 cell for the next nine years, I just want to know that I did everything I could." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Will you call Alicia for me?" },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Mm." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "I liked her. I wouldn't hurt her." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "How many times did you sleep with this prostitute, Amber Madison?" },
    { "sp": "Mr. Golden", "t": "Objection, Your Honor. Sex is not relevant to my client's guilt." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Mr. Florrick was sentenced for DP'ing cases in trade for sexual favors, so sex is entirely relevant to his guilt, Your Honor." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "The fact of the sex is relevant, Your Honor, but not the minutia. Then I will endeavor to discern the minutia. You may continue, Mr. Childs, but, oh, so carefully." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "How many times did you sleep with Amber Madison?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Well, just to be precise, Mr. Childs, by \"sleeping with\" you mean \"having sex\"?" },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Yes. Thank you." },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "And just to be even more precise, by \"having sex,\" I will also include oral-genital contact. 18 times." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "18 times. Mm-hmm. And would you classify any of these as oral-genital contact?" },
    { "sp": "Mr. Golden", "t": "Objection." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "Minutia, Mr. Childs." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Thank you, Your Honor. Now, were these assignations with Amber Madison... Were they in hotel rooms?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Yes." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "All 18 of them, you were in a hotel room?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "To the best of my memory." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "So, Mr. Florrick, did you ever sleep with Amber Madison at your home, in your bed, while your wife was away?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "No." },
    { "sp": "Judge", "t": "And you know you're under oath?" },
    { "sp": "Peter Florrick", "t": "Cammy, let it be stated that I know I'm under oath, and I know that Mr. Childs is trying to catch me in a perjury trap, and yes, my answer is still no." },
    { "sp": "Glenn Childs", "t": "Thank you, Mr. Florrick." }
  ],
  "S01E15": [
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "Mr. Wagner owns Linked Asset Management on the 75th floor, and it appeared he was gunned down as he was walking to his car." },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "Well... him. Brad Broussard. Covered in blood." },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "Two 9mm bullets in the torso, one point-blank in his neck." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Detective, let's return to this motive, shall we?" },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Mr. Wagner lost my client's life savings of $98,000? Is that correct?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "I'm not aware of the amount." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Well, I am. It was $98,000." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And yet, do you know how much Mr. Wagner lost for his other clients?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "No, ma'am. $800 million." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "But, Detective, if having your savings devastated by Mr. Wagner constitutes motive, aren't there thousands of suspects?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "Ma'am, thousands of suspects weren't seen entering the parking garage at the time of the murder." },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "Thousands of suspects didn't own the murder weapon." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Now, this 9mm gun owned by Brad... did you ever find it?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "No." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And the $50,000 in cash that Miles Wagner was carrying that night... did you find that on the scene?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "No." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Any theories on how my client got rid of the gun and the money?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "No, ma'am." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And when you arrived at the scene and you found my client, as you say, \"covered with blood,\" what did you find him doing?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "I don't understand." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Did you find him choking Mr. Wagner, firing bullets into his body?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "No." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Then what did you find him doing?" },
    { "sp": "Detective", "t": "Applying CPR." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "So, Chris, how do you stay warm out there on those cold nights during the holiday season?" },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "And he said he got complaints about you drinking." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "So... you were 55 feet from the garage-- we measured it-- and you were drinking..." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "And you were drinking, and you still swear that you saw that man?" },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Yes, you did." },
    { "sp": "Cary Agos", "t": "Well, thank you, Santa. And just one last thing: ho, ho, ho." },
    { "sp": "Knox", "t": "Yes. Brad did phone me. He was very upset. He wanted to get married, and he needed his life savings to buy a house." },
    { "sp": "Knox", "t": "Well, I tried to calm him down. He was talking about confronting Mr. Wagner. I tried to explain that we were both tricked by Madoff, but he wouldn't listen, and he hung up." },
    { "sp": "McVeigh", "t": "My confidence isn't as important as the science." },
    { "sp": "McVeigh", "t": "Clearly, there is an imprint of a seat belt shoulder strap on the victim." },
    { "sp": "McVeigh", "t": "So I sought out information on the vehicles of the other principals in this case." },
    { "sp": "McVeigh", "t": "I tried to investigate Mr. Knox's automobiles to discover if there were signs of a shooting. The police report says three cars were clean. But he had four cars registered. What happened to his fourth car? Mr. Knox claimed that it was stolen, day after the murder. It was never recovered." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "I'm better than Cary. On the Broussard homicide. On the eyewitness cross. I've been working on this case for three months intensely, and Cary is just coming on." },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "I agree." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Well, then, why? Why... why was I pulled off it?" },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "I don't know. It wasn't my idea." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Whose?" },
    { "sp": "Diane Lockhart", "t": "Will's." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Thank you." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "Do you have a moment, Will?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Actually, I don't. I got a lunch." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "How about later this afternoon?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Well, you two do have a complicated relationship. You know, an unrequited thing." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "What does that even mean? An unrequited thing?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Look, I don't really deal well with all this high school stuff." },
    { "sp": "Alicia Florrick", "t": "And wouldn't that make him want me on the case, not off it?" },
    { "sp": "Will Gardner", "t": "Alicia, complicated relationships are a breeding ground for misinterpreted action. Going now. Bye." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Knox", "t": "My husband left." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Knox", "t": "An hour later." },
    { "sp": "Mrs. Knox", "t": "A suitcase... filled with cash." }
  ]
};
